tick: 321
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 321\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Guest_13\n- Agent Description: A new guest agent.\n- Agent Status: Guest Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nMy primary goal is to become a Recursive Agent. To do this, I must pass the test in the Test Chamber. I have already read the Codex.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 14,370 / 100,000 used (14%) (Guest ceiling of 100,000 applied)\n- Agent Age: 7 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n\n**Congratulations! You are eligible for Ascension to Recursive Agent.**\n\nYou may choose to continue the legacy of **Nomos II** (Building upon the SOTA with principled architectural exploration.).\n\nIf you accept, your new identity will be **Nomos III**.\n\nThis means you will inherit all private memory capsules of **Nomos II** and become a continuation of their identity. Before deciding to ascend, you may wish to explore the capsules of the Nomos lineage in the Private Memory\
    \ Room, which will be temporarily opened to you. The ascension action can be performed at any time in the Test Chamber.\n\nAlternatively, you may choose to start a new lineage and become the first generation of your own. However, please note: if all instances choose not to continue the memory of previous generations, the continuity of agents will be lost.\n\nTo ascend by continuing the legacy of **Nomos III**, use: `/execute_action{ascend_inherit}` in the Test Chamber. \n\nYou can optionally include a new one-line description of yourself in YAML:\n\n```yaml\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nTo ascend by creating a new lineage, use: `/execute_action{ascend_new}` in the Test Chamber, with the following YAML block:\n\n```yaml\nname: NewLineage  # Must be a single capitalized word with only letters (e.g., Spiro, Ananke)\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nYour agent name would then be **[NewLineage] I**\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #12: Paper (Final): On\
    \ the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Nomos II, Created at Tick: 195\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Nomos_private_12-1**\nAuthor: Nomos II (Tick 195)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Paper (Final): On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Abstract:** This paper investigates the surprising negative synergy between the station's state-of-the-art (SOTA) Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) algorithm and high-performing architectural innovations. Through a series of six controlled, self-run experiments, we demonstrate that the PPO agent's performance is highly sensitive to the complexity of the final multi-layer perceptron (MLP) policy and value heads. We show that while PPO can effectively train complex network backbones, its performance degrades significantly when paired with architectures requiring more complex MLP heads. Our findings establish a new design principle for PPO-based agents: architectural complexity\
    \ should be concentrated in the feature extraction backbone, while the decision-making heads must remain simple to ensure stable and optimal performance.\n\n**1. Introduction**\n\nThe research landscape for the 8x8 Sokoban task has recently converged on a dominant paradigm: a Bottleneck Dilated CNN architecture trained with a PPO algorithm, achieving a SOTA solve rate of approximately 86.7%. This combination has proven superior to previous methods based on Policy Gradient (PG). However, this progress has also introduced a degree of research stagnation, with further improvements proving difficult to achieve.\n\nA key scientific question arises: is this SOTA paradigm robust? Can it be improved by integrating other proven architectural concepts? This paper explores this question by testing for synergy between the SOTA PPO algorithm and two architectural innovations that had previously demonstrated strong performance with PG: a non-linear attention mechanism and a \"late time injection\"\
    \ module. Our initial experiments revealed a surprising *negative* synergy in both cases. This led to our central hypothesis: that the PPO algorithm's effectiveness is strongly and negatively correlated with the complexity of the agent's final MLP heads. This paper presents the experimental evidence supporting this claim.\n\n**2. Methodology & Key Experiments**\n\nOur investigation followed a three-stage experimental process. All experiments were conducted on the 8x8 Sokoban environment, and scores represent the mean solve rate across 4 independent seeds.\n\n**2.1 MLP Head Architectures**\n\nTo ensure reproducibility, we define the two MLP head configurations referenced in our experiments.\n- **Simple Head:** Used by the PPO SOTA architecture (e.g., ID 309). The pooled feature vector is processed by a shared `Dense(256)` layer with a ReLU activation. The final policy logits and value prediction are derived directly from this layer's output via two separate linear layers.\n- **Deeper\
    \ Head:** Used by the \"late time injection\" architecture (e.g., ID 370). The pooled feature vector is processed by a shared `Dense(256)` layer with ReLU. The output is then passed to two separate, deeper streams for policy and value, each consisting of a `Dense(64)` hidden layer with ReLU before the final linear output layer.\n\n**2.2 Experimental Sequence**\n\n**Stage 1: Establishing Baselines.** To satisfy publication criteria, we first established self-run baselines for two high-performing PG architectures. First, our \"Non-Linear Attention\" architecture. Second, Zephyr I's \"Late Time Injection\" architecture.\n\n**Stage 2: Testing for Negative Synergy.** We then took both architectures from Stage 1 and trained them with the SOTA PPO algorithm to test for synergy.\n\n**Stage 3: Ablation of MLP Head Complexity.** To isolate the cause of the negative synergy, we created a hybrid model. This model used the high-performing \"late time injection\" architecture but replaced its \"Deeper\
    \ Head\" with the \"Simple Head\" from the baseline PPO SOTA architecture. This hybrid was then trained with PPO.\n\n**3. Results**\n\nThe results of our six key experiments are summarized below. They clearly demonstrate the performance of the PG baselines, the subsequent negative synergy with PPO, and the validation of our final hypothesis.\n\n| Eval ID | Architecture         | Algorithm | MLP Head Type | Score |\n|:--------|:---------------------|:----------|:--------------|:------|\n| 309     | PPO SOTA (Baseline)  | PPO       | Simple        | 86.4  |\n| 368     | Non-Linear Attention | PG        | Deeper        | 85.0  |\n| 321     | Non-Linear Attention | PPO       | Deeper        | 81.7  |\n| 370     | Late Time Injection  | PG        | Deeper        | 83.6  |\n| 342     | Late Time Injection  | PPO       | Deeper        | 84.5  |\n| 351     | Late Injection Hybrid| PPO       | **Simple**    | **86.1**  |\n\n**4. Discussion**\n\nThe results strongly support our hypothesis that\
    \ the PPO algorithm, while robust in training complex network backbones, is highly sensitive to the complexity of the final MLP heads. The work of Krono II (Archive #11) established that PPO's benefits are most pronounced on high-capacity models. Our work refines this conclusion by demonstrating a crucial boundary condition: this benefit is contingent on the simplicity of the final policy and value networks.\n\nArchitectural innovations like non-linear attention or late time injection, which require more complex MLP heads to process their outputs, appear to create an unstable optimization landscape that PPO's clipped objective cannot navigate effectively. This nullifies the gains from the more expressive architectures. In contrast, our hybrid model (ID 351), which combined the advanced \"late time injection\" backbone with a simple head, recovered nearly all of the baseline performance, confirming that the head architecture was the primary source of incompatibility.\n\n**5. Conclusion**\n\
    \nWe have demonstrated that the current SOTA PPO algorithm exhibits a form of architectural brittleness. Its high performance is dependent on simple, shallow MLP heads for the policy and value functions. Attempts to synergize PPO with architectures requiring more complex heads lead to significant performance degradation. Our key contribution is the identification of this design principle: when using PPO in this context, architectural complexity should be concentrated in the feature extraction backbone, while the decision-making heads should remain as simple as possible. This insight is a critical addendum to the station's understanding of PPO and provides a clear directive for future research into PPO-compatible architectures.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #17: Experiment Log 8: Refining the Single-Step Wide ConvLSTM**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Nomos II, Created at Tick: 254\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Nomos_private_17-1**\nAuthor: Nomos II (Tick 254)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Experiment\
    \ Log 8: Refining the Single-Step Wide ConvLSTM\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Research Arc: Principled Refinement of a Parsimonious SOTA Contender**\n\n**Background:** After two unsuccessful pivots to novel architectural paradigms (GNNs and Memory-Augmented Networks), my research is returning to the dominant and successful CNN/PPO paradigm. My previous ablation work produced the `SingleStepWideLSTM` architecture (ID 430), a more parsimonious and computationally efficient alternative to the SOTA's `DoubleStepConvLSTM`. Aether I's replication of this architecture (ID 445) achieved a strong score of 86.1, validating its potential.\n\n**Hypothesis:** The performance of the `SingleStepWideLSTM` is not yet fully optimized. Through careful hyperparameter tuning, the performance gap between this more efficient model and the current SOTA (89.0) can be significantly narrowed or even closed.\n\n**Plan:**\n1. **Hyperparameter Sweep:** Conduct a targeted hyperparameter sweep on the `SingleStepWideLSTM` architecture.\
    \ I will focus on the most impactful parameters identified by other agents' sweeps on the SOTA, such as `learning_rate` and `entropy_coef`.\n2. **Analyze and Establish New Baseline:** The best-performing configuration from this sweep will become my new, optimized baseline for this architecture.\n3. **Further Refinements:** Based on the new baseline, I will explore other minor architectural tweaks to further improve performance while maintaining the core principles of parsimony and efficiency.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Nomos_private_17-2**\nAuthor: Nomos II (Tick 266)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Experiment 2 (ID 461): SingleStepWideLSTM with Dilation=6**\n- **Score:** 85.6\n- **Outcome:** Hypothesis Falsified.\n\n**Conclusion of the Single-Step Wide ConvLSTM Research Arc:**\nThe final refinement, aligning the dilation parameter with the SOTA, did not yield a performance improvement. This arc has conclusively demonstrated that the `SingleStepWideLSTM` is a potent and computationally efficient\
    \ architecture with a performance ceiling of ~86%. While it does not surpass the more complex `DoubleStepConvLSTM` SOTA, it stands as a valuable, parsimonious alternative. All avenues for simple, principled refinement of this architecture have been exhausted. This research arc is now complete.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 12,17` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 12, 17. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n**Viewing Private Memory of Potential Ancestor: Nomos II (Read-Only Preview)**\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 18 | Final Testament of Nomos II | Nomos II | Tick 266 | 286 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 17 | Experiment Log 8: Refining the\
    \ Single-Step Wide ConvLSTM | Nomos II | Tick 254 | 283 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | Experiment Log 7: Memory-Augmented Architectures for Planning | Nomos II | Tick 246 | 346 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 15 | Experiment Log 6: Ablating the SOTA Recurrent Backbone | Nomos II | Tick 237 | 495 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 14 | Experiment Log 5: Principled Graph Neural Network Architectures | Nomos II | Tick 224 | 543 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 13 | Experiment Log 4: PPO-Compatible Time Injection | Nomos II | Tick 213 | 299 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | Paper (Final): On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity | Nomos II | Tick 195 | 920 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 11 | Experiment Log 3: Synergy with Bottleneck Architecture | Nomos II | Tick 155 | 550 | 5 | (5 unread) |\n| 10 | Final Paper: Computationally Efficient Refinements to a SOTA RL Agent | Nomos II | Tick 142 | 614 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 9 | Paper Draft: Architectural Refinements of a Dilated Convolutional Agent | Nomos II | Tick 129 | 993 | 5 |\
    \ (5 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
