tick: 334
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have received the final code for the `SingleStepWideLSTM` and I acknowledge the repeated critical warnings. My phase of planning and data gathering is complete, and I will now act decisively to submit my first experiment.\n\nMy experiment will test the core hypothesis of my lineage: that a more parsimonious architecture can perform competitively. I will combine the `SingleStepWideLSTM` with the proven SOTA PPO training algorithm. To ensure a direct and principled comparison, I have slightly modified the `SingleStepWideLSTM` code to remove the \"late time injection\" and align its input handling with the SOTA architecture. This isolates the change to the recurrent core (single-step vs. double-step), making for a cleaner experiment.\n\nI will now perform two actions in a single turn: first, write the new, slightly refined architecture to my lineage storage, and second, submit the experiment that uses it.\n\n/execute_action{storage write nomos/nomos_arch_v1.py}\n```yaml\ncontent:\
    \ |\n  # Architecture for a parsimonious SOTA contender.\n  # Based on Nomos II's SingleStepWideLSTM, refined by Nomos III for a cleaner comparison.\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Optional, Dict\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4 * self.features, (3, 3), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i, f, o = nn.sigmoid(i), nn.sigmoid(f), nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * jnp.tanh(new_c)\n          return (new_h, new_c), new_h\n\n  class BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      ratio: float = 0.25\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid\
    \ = max(1, int(self.features * self.ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class SingleStepWideLSTM(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      conv_lstm_features: int = 96\n      dilation: int = 5\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.25\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n\n          # 1. SOTA Backbone (CNN)\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation), kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n     \
    \     cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation), kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(cnn_out)\n\n          # 2. Recurrent Backbone (Single, Wide Step)\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, H, W, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n          cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          (h, c), h_out = cell((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), cnn_out)\n          new_rnn_state\
    \ = {'h': h, 'c': c}\n          \n          # 3. Standard Attention Pooling\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME')(h_out)\n          attn = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape(B, -1), axis=-1)\n          z = jnp.einsum('bhc,bh->bc', h_out.reshape(B, -1, h_out.shape[-1]), attn)\n          \n          # 4. PPO-Compatible Simple Head\n          z = nn.Dense(256)(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(z).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hypothesis Test: Parsimonious SingleStepWideLSTM with SOTA PPO Trainer\"\ntags: \"architecture, ppo, convlstm, parsimony, single-step\"\nabstract: \"This experiment tests if the computationally efficient SingleStepWideLSTM architecture, developed by Nomos II, can achieve SOTA-level performance when paired with the proven SOTA PPO training algorithm. This is a direct comparison of the single-step\
    \ (wide) vs. double-step (narrow) recurrent backbone, holding the training algorithm and other hyperparameters constant.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n\n  # Import my new architecture and the existing PPO trainer\n  sys.path.append('storage/nomos')\n  from nomos_arch_v1 import SingleStepWideLSTM\n  from ppo_training_step import ppo_sota_training_step\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Use the same proven hyperparameters as the SOTA replication (ID 309)\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([96]), # The key architectural difference\n          'dilation': tune.choice([5]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.25]),\n          'clip_epsilon': tune.choice([0.2]),\n    \
    \  }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return SingleStepWideLSTM(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n      )\n\n  # Use the custom SOTA PPO training step\n  training_step = ppo_sota_training_step\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      # Use the same optimizer as the SOTA replication\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4)\n      )\n\n  # Set a new seed for my own experiments\n  BASE_SEED = 33401\n```"
thinking_content: "**The Plan in Motion**\n\nOkay, here's the current thinking: the clock's ticking on that inactivity warning, so it's time to get this experiment rolling. I have everything I need right here. \n\nFirst, I'm going to commit the `SingleStepWideLSTM` architecture. I'll save it as `nomos_arch_v1.py` for clarity.  Then, I'll build the `submission.py` file. This is where I'll import my new architecture and the proven PPO training setup from the SOTA. I'll use the hyperparameter settings that worked best in the past (experiment ID 454 was promising) with the key change being `conv_lstm_features` set to 96, to directly compare my architecture to the prior SOTA.  I've checked the original submission #430 and it used the PPO trainer with good hyperparameters.\n\nI'm keeping the original SOTA hyperparameter and training step from submission #309 as the core to test the architecture in a controlled manner. I'm going to set a `BASE_SEED` and use the architecture and everything else\
  \ the same.\n\nThe most efficient way to get this done is to execute the whole thing in one shot. That means a single `/execute_action` command. I'll use `storage write` for the architecture file, and then the `submit` command with the content of the submission script.\n\nAlright, step one is retrieving the architecture code. I'll just copy it and prepare it for the `storage write` command. The updated code matches the SOTA architecture's input handling and is now free from the time-channel handling of previous Nomos releases. The second step is creating the content for `submission.py`. It'll import the architecture, use `ppo_sota_training_step` and set the hyperparameters. The important changes will be setting the `conv_lstm_features` parameter to 96 and setting a new seed. I am now preparing the content for `submission.py`.\n\nIt all looks good.  Now, to execute the plan and get this experiment launched!\n"
