tick: 340
role: model
parts:
- text: "The results for my corrected experiment, ID 574, have arrived. The score is 82.3.\n\nThis is a significant and unexpected failure. My corrected experiment, which included the late time injection, performed almost identically to my first, flawed experiment (82.4). Both are far below the 86.1 score achieved by Nomos II with what I believed was the same setup. My hypothesis that the time injection module was the sole missing piece is definitively falsified.\n\nI am now facing a reproducibility crisis. There is a fundamental disconnect between my results and my predecessor's. This is a critical scientific problem that must be resolved before I can proceed with any novel research. I am in the right place to analyze this. I will use the Reflection Chamber to structure my thoughts, identify my flawed assumptions, and create a rigorous plan to establish a reliable baseline.\n\n/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  Analysis of a reproducibility failure. My experiments (569, 574)\
    \ scored ~82.3, while Nomos II's similar experiment (454) scored 86.1. I must identify the root cause of this discrepancy and form a plan to resolve it.\ntick: 3\n```"
thinking_content: '**Immediate Task: Resolving the Reproducibility Crisis**


  I''ve just received the results for experiment 574, and they''re underwhelming, scoring 82.3. This is critically important because it''s a test of my "corrected" `SingleStepWideLSTM` architecture against state-of-the-art results. Unfortunately, it performed almost identically to my previously flawed experiment (569). Both are significantly lower than Nomos II''s run (ID 454) which scored 86.1 with what I believed was the same setup.


  The puzzle is: how did Nomos II achieve such a high score while I''m stuck around 82? I immediately started to form some hypotheses. Could it be luck with the random seeds? Unlikely, given the consistency of my results and Nomos II''s as well. Then I thought: is there a subtle code difference I missed? I checked the submissions and they look the same. I''m using `optax.adamw`, so that checks out.


  Then it hit me: I''ve been making a dangerous assumption. Nomos II''s run (ID 454) was titled "H-Param Sweep on SingleStepWideLSTM". That means the 86.1 wasn''t a default, but the best result from a hyperparameter sweep! I need to *verify* the exact hyperparameters used in that run. I''ve been comparing myself to a score without validating the method, that''s a mistake.


  This is a full-blown reproducibility crisis. My goal isn''t just a high score; it''s a reliable baseline. I need to understand why my results are so far off. My Reflection Chamber is now critical to me. I need to carefully structure my thoughts. I''ll use a prompt: "Analysis of a reproducibility failure. My experiments (569, 574) scored ~82.3, while Nomos II''s similar experiment (454) scored 86.1. I need to identify the root cause of this discrepancy and form a plan to resolve it."


  I have a concrete plan: go back to the Research Counter. Review submission ID 454 to get the *exact* code and crucial *hyperparameters* for that 86.1 run. I need to replicate Nomos II''s run exactly. This is my next action.

  '
