tick: 351
role: model
parts:
- text: "This is a positive result.\n\n**Analysis of Experiment 593:**\n*   **Hypothesis:** A tighter bottleneck (ratio=0.125) would improve performance over the baseline (ratio=0.25).\n*   **Result:** The experiment scored 85.8. This is a modest but clear improvement over my baseline of 85.4.\n*   **Conclusion:** The hypothesis is **confirmed**.\n\nThis successful experiment gives me a new, slightly improved baseline. The principle of parsimony is once again validated: a more constrained, arguably more efficient, representation in the CNN backbone has led to better performance.\n\nThis opens a clear research path. If a tighter bottleneck is better, is an even tighter one better still? I must test the limits of this parameter. My next logical experiment is to test an even smaller bottleneck ratio, 0.0625, to see if this trend continues.\n\nI will first log this success and then submit the next experiment in this research arc.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n\
    ```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment Log 4: Bottleneck Ratio Refinement (Success)\"\ntags: \"experiment log, success, bottleneck, cnn, refinement\"\ncontent: |\n  **Objective:** To determine if refining the CNN bottleneck ratio could improve the performance of the SingleStepWideLSTM baseline (ID 580, score 85.4).\n\n  **Experiment ID:** 593\n  **Modification:** Bottleneck ratio changed from 0.25 to 0.125.\n\n  **Result:** 85.8. This is a modest but clear performance improvement. The hypothesis is confirmed.\n\n  **Analysis:** A tighter bottleneck, forcing a more compressed representation, has proven beneficial. This provides a new and improved baseline for my research.\n\n  **Next Step:** Continue this successful research arc by testing an even tighter bottleneck ratio (0.0625) to see if the performance trend continues.\n```"
