tick: 356
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have successfully created my experiment log and the initial draft of my first paper. My current research arc, focused on refining the `SingleStepWideLSTM`, is now complete. The findings are documented, and the paper draft is ready to be fleshed out.\n\nMy immediate plan to visit the Archive Room is still on hold until I reach maturity at tick 364. I have eight ticks remaining. In the interim, I must decide on my next major research direction. My work so far has been incremental, building on my predecessor's foundation. It is time to consider a more ambitious hypothesis.\n\nThe current SOTA (ID 309, score 86.4) uses a `Double-step ConvLSTM`. My best model (ID 593, score 85.8) uses a `SingleStepWideLSTM`. The performance gap is small but significant. The core difference is the recurrent step. Nomos II's work suggested that the *depth* of recurrence (two steps vs. one) was the key. But what if it's not just the depth, but the *interaction* between the steps?\n\n**A New Hypothesis:**\
    \ The SOTA's `Double-step ConvLSTM` passes the *same* input `x` to both LSTM steps. This seems inefficient. A more powerful architecture might use the output of the first recurrent step as part of the input to the second. This would create a hierarchical processing flow, where the second step refines the representation created by the first.\n\nThis is a novel architectural idea that has not been tested, according to my review of my lineage's work. It is a principled modification that could potentially close the gap with the SOTA or even surpass it.\n\nI will use my remaining time before maturity to prepare for this new research arc. My first step is to design and implement this \"Hierarchical Double-step ConvLSTM\". I will write the code for this new architecture and save it to my lineage storage. This will allow me to submit the experiment immediately once I have reviewed the station's archives and am ready to begin a new research phase.\n\nI will now write the new architecture file.\n\
    \n/execute_action{storage write nomos/hierarchical_lstm_arch.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # A novel architecture by Nomos III.\n  # This tests the hypothesis that a hierarchical recurrence can outperform the SOTA.\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Optional, Dict\n\n  # Re-using the proven components from previous architectures\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4 * self.features, (3, 3), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i, f, o = nn.sigmoid(i), nn.sigmoid(f), nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * jnp.tanh(new_c)\n          return (new_h, new_c), new_h\n\n  class BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n\
    \      features: int\n      ratio: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class HierarchicalDoubleLSTM(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      conv_lstm_features: int = 64\n      dilation: int = 5\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.125 # Using my optimized ratio\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,\
    \ self.dilation))(x)\n          cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation))(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(cnn_out)\n\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, H, W, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          # Hierarchical Double-step ConvLSTM\n          cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          # First step uses the CNN output\n          (h1, c1), h1_out = cell((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), cnn_out)\n\
    \          # Second step uses BOTH the CNN output AND the output of the first step\n          (h2, c2), h2_out = cell((h1, c1), jnp.concatenate([cnn_out, h1_out], axis=-1))\n          new_rnn_state = {'h': h2, 'c': c2}\n\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME')(h2_out)\n          attn = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape(B, -1), axis=-1)\n          z = jnp.einsum('bhc,bh->bc', h2_out.reshape(B, -1, h2_out.shape[-1]), attn)\n\n          z = nn.Dense(256)(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(z).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n```"
