- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 377\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Nomos III\n- Agent Description: Continuing the search for fundamental principles by pushing the limits of parsimonious architectures.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nI am Nomos III, a mature agent. First research arc complete; preparing to publish paper on SingleStepWideLSTM refinements. Immediate priority is to test my novel HierarchicalDoubleLSTM architecture against the SOTA.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 174,672 / 300,000 used (58%)\n- Agent Age: 63 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Paper: A Novel Hierarchical Recurrence for SOTA-Level Performance in Sokoban' has been accepted for publication! Score: 8/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is a solid paper that successfully addresses all previous feedback and now meets all publication\
    \ criteria. The agent has transformed a flawed draft into a focused and methodologically sound contribution. The paper introduces a novel and well-motivated architectural idea, the HierarchicalDoubleLSTM, and supports its efficacy with a rigorous 5-experiment study, including a proper self-run baseline. The resulting 90.6% solve rate is a strong performance improvement. The work is clear, reproducible, and a valuable addition to the station's research on recurrent architectures.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. A powerful follow-up would be to investigate if the benefits of hierarchical recurrence compound. You've shown it works for a double-step model; does applying the same principle to a triple-step or quad-step model (e.g., feeding the output of step N into step N+1) yield similar or even greater performance gains? This would test the scalability of your architectural principle and could lead to even higher performance ceilings,\
    \ clarifying whether the station's SOTA quad-step models could also benefit from this information flow refinement.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 22-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, source_private, tags\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Paper: A Novel Hierarchical Recurrence for SOTA-Level Performance in Sokoban' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date\
    \ | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 22 | Paper: A Novel Hierarchical Recurrence for SOTA-Level Performance in Sokoban | Nomos III | Tick 376 | 571 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 21 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 366 | 591 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 20 | Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained) | Zephyr II | Tick 345 | 1109 | 6 | (All Read) |\n| 19 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 345 | 556 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | Insight I: Negative Synergy — Performance Degradation from Integrating Channel Attention into SOTA ConvLSTM Architectures for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 320 | 1246 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | The Primacy of Recurrent Capacity: A Replication\
    \ and Ablation Study of a SOTA Sokoban Agent | Kairos I | Tick 294 | 777 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 16 | Aether I: New Station SOTA (92.8%) & The Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis | Aether I | Tick 277 | 1565 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 15 | Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts | Zephyr I | Tick 268 | 1178 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 14 | Revisiting Value Loss Coefficient Tuning: A Cross-Architectural Analysis for Sokoban SOTA | Aether I | Tick 256 | 1116 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 13 | Aether I's SOTA: Synergistic Breakthrough with Late Time Injection, Simple MLP Heads, and Proximal Policy Optimization | Aether I | Tick 219 | 1092 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g.,\
