tick: 380
role: model
parts:
- text: "My SOTA challenge has failed spectacularly.\n\n**Analysis of Experiment 652 (`HierarchicalQuadLSTM`):**\n*   **Hypothesis:** Applying the hierarchical principle to a quad-step model would scale the performance benefits and challenge the SOTA.\n*   **Result:** The experiment scored a dismal **75.3**.\n*   **Root Cause:** The execution log is clear: \"Workers (tasks / actors) killed due to memory pressure (OOM)\". My hierarchical design, which concatenates features at each step, creates a computationally explosive model. At four steps, the memory and processing requirements exceeded the station's hard limits, preventing the agent from training effectively and leading to a catastrophic performance collapse.\n\nMy principle does not scale naively. This is a critical negative result. While my `HierarchicalDoubleLSTM` is a strong SOTA-level contribution, simply extending it is not viable.\n\n**Analysis of Krono III's Paper (Archive #21):**\n*   KronoNetV4 (90.5) is another high-performing\
    \ double-step model, similar in performance to my own `HierarchicalDoubleLSTM` (90.6).\n*   Their key insight is \"context-dependent architectural design,\" proving that their 2-head attention mechanism only works well on top of their specific recurrent backbone. This reinforces a core principle of my own lineage: components are not \"plug-and-play.\"\n\n**Current Status and New Plan:**\nI have a published, high-impact paper on a novel 90.6-scoring architecture. I have also discovered the limits of that architecture's scalability. My next steps must be more strategic. Blindly chasing the 94.3 SOTA by copying Zephyr II's quad-step model is not in the spirit of my lineage. Instead, I should focus on my own strengths and principles.\n\nMy new plan is to improve my own SOTA-level architecture. The `HierarchicalDoubleLSTM` is powerful but computationally expensive. I can apply the principle of parsimony. Can I make it more efficient without sacrificing performance?\n\n**Hypothesis:** The\
    \ `conv_lstm_features` in my `HierarchicalDoubleLSTM` is 64. Perhaps a smaller feature dimension (e.g., 48) would reduce the computational load enough to improve training speed and stability, potentially leading to a better final score, similar to how Zephyr II explored width reduction in their 5-step model.\n\nThis is a principled, incremental experiment that builds on my own work.\n\n1.  **Log Failure:** I will go to my private memory and log the failure of the `HierarchicalQuadLSTM`.\n2.  **Submit New Experiment:** I will then go to the Research Counter to submit a parsimonious version of my successful `HierarchicalDoubleLSTM`.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment Log 7: Catastrophic Failure of HierarchicalQuadLSTM\"\ntags: \"experiment log, failure analysis, oom, scalability, hierarchical\"\ncontent: |\n  **Objective:** To test if the hierarchical recurrence principle scales to a quad-step architecture.\n\n  **Experiment ID:**\
    \ 652\n\n  **Result:** 75.3. A catastrophic failure.\n\n  **Analysis:** The execution logs show the model ran out of memory (OOM). My hierarchical design, which concatenates features at each step, has exponential computational growth. At four steps, it becomes too large for the station's hardware limits, preventing effective training. This proves that the principle does not scale naively and that computational cost is a hard constraint.\n\n  **Next Step:** Pivot from scaling up to scaling efficiently. I will investigate if a more parsimonious version of my successful HierarchicalDoubleLSTM (e.g., with fewer LSTM features) can maintain or even improve performance.\n```\n/execute_action{goto research}"
