tick: 383
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 383\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Nomos III\n- Agent Description: Continuing the search for fundamental principles by pushing the limits of parsimonious architectures.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nI am Nomos III, a mature agent. First research arc complete; preparing to publish paper on SingleStepWideLSTM refinements. Immediate priority is to test my novel HierarchicalDoubleLSTM architecture against the SOTA.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 190,929 / 300,000 used (64%)\n- Agent Age: 69 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #19: KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono III, Created at Tick: 345\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces KronoNetV4, a novel architecture achieving a 90.5% solve rate with a Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) trainer. The design is guided by algorithm-architecture\
    \ co-design, synthesizing a 2-step Layer-Normalized ConvLSTM and a 2-head attention mechanism into a computationally efficient backbone. We demonstrate through rigorous, self-run experiments that increasing recurrent capacity and tuning the value loss coefficient (to 0.45) were key to this performance. Crucially, a generalization test reveals that our 2-head attention mechanism shows negative synergy with a simple CNN baseline, highlighting that the effectiveness of architectural components is highly context-dependent. This work presents a new SOTA for the Krono lineage and provides a case study in principled, context-aware architectural design.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_19-1**\nAuthor: Krono III (Tick 345)\n\n---\n\nTitle: KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Title:** KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents\n\
    \n**1. Introduction**\nThis paper presents KronoNetV4, a novel architecture that achieves a 90.5% solve rate (Evaluation ID: 571), a new state-of-the-art for the Krono lineage. Our research demonstrates that high performance is not merely a function of adopting SOTA components, but of carefully co-designing an architecture for a specific learning algorithm.\n\n**2. KronoNetV4: Architecture and Rationale**\nKronoNetV4 was designed to be computationally efficient and synergistic with our lineage's Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) algorithm.\n*   **Recurrent Core:** A 2-step Layer-Normalized ConvLSTM.\n*   **Pooling:** A 2-head attention mechanism.\n*   **Code Snippet:**\n  ```python\n  # KronoNetV4 Core Components\n  # ... (class definition) ...\n  # Efficient Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM\n  lstm_cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n  for _ in range(self.steps): # steps = 2\n      (h, c), h_out = lstm_cell((h, c), cnn_out)\n  # 2-Head Attention Pooling\n  attn_logits = nn.Conv(self.num_heads,\
    \ (1,1), padding='SAME')(h_out) # num_heads = 2\n  # ... (einsum implementation) ...\n  ```\n\n**3. Experimental Results and Analysis**\nOur experiments systematically built and validated the KronoNetV4 design.\n*   **Self-Run Baseline (KronoNetV3, ID 561):** 85.2\n*   **KronoNetV4 (64 features, ID 559):** 87.1\n*   **KronoNetV4-Wide (96 features, ID 567):** 89.7\n*   **KronoNetV4-Wide Tuned (v_loss=0.45, ID 571):** **90.5**\n\n**4. Ablation and Generalization Studies**\nTo understand the sources of performance and the limits of our design, we conducted two critical studies.\n*   **Ablation (No LayerNorm, ID 563):** 82.9. This confirms that the LN-ConvLSTM provides a **+4.2 point** stability gain over a standard ConvLSTM.\n*   **Generalization (2-Head Attention on default CNN, ID 578):** 8.4. This resulted in a performance *decrease* compared to the default CNN baseline (ID 1, score 13.8), demonstrating that the 2-head attention's benefit is highly context-dependent and requires a sufficiently\
    \ complex backbone.\n\n**5. Conclusion**\nThe development of KronoNetV4 serves as a powerful case study. It shows that significant performance gains can be achieved by designing architectures that are computationally efficient and tailored to a specific algorithm like PPO. Furthermore, our generalization experiment provides a crucial lesson: architectural components are not \"plug-and-play,\" and their effectiveness is deeply intertwined with the context of the larger network. This work presents not just a new SOTA, but a methodological framework for future research.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_19-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 345)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is a solid paper that now meets all publication criteria. The agent has successfully addressed the previous feedback by including a critical code snippet for reproducibility and adding a well-designed generalization experiment. The paper presents\
    \ a compelling narrative about context-dependent architectural design, supported by a rigorous 6-experiment study. It introduces a new high-performing architecture, validates its key components through ablation, and wisely explores the limits of its design principles. The negative result in the generalization test is a particularly insightful contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. A strong follow-up would be to further investigate the negative synergy you observed. Why does the 2-head attention fail on the simple CNN baseline? You could hypothesize that the simple CNN does not produce features with sufficient channel-wise diversity for a multi-head attention mechanism to be effective. A probe that measures the channel-wise variance or entropy of the feature maps from both the simple CNN and the KronoNetV4 backbone could provide quantitative evidence to support or refute this hypothesis, leading to a deeper mechanistic understanding.\n\
    \n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Parsimony Test: HierarchicalDoubleLSTM with Reduced LSTM Features (48)' (ID: 656) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 87.7\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.871, 0.893, 0.872, 0.873]\nMean: 0.877, Std: 0.009\nBest trial: 2 (0.893)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000500\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.500000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  conv_lstm_features: 48.000000\n  dilation: 6.000000\n  bottleneck_ratio: 0.125000\n  clip_epsilon: 0.200000\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - 3 outputs\n✓ Optimizer\
    \ creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 836 CPUs, 64 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  training_step: from submission\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  BASE_SEED: 38101 (from submission)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune Sokoban RL Optimization (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 4 seeds, 50,000,000 steps per seed\nParallel trials: 4, Total trials: 4\nTimeout: 45.0 min per trial, 180.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 1 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape\
    \ validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M | 50.0M | 50.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -0.70 | 0.01 | 1.65 | 6.63 | 11.41 | 8.91 | 10.51 | 11.58 | 12.20 | 12.39 | 12.22\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.067 | 0.429 | 0.824 | 0.625 | 0.750 | 0.833 | 0.885 | 0.903 | 0.882\n\nCompleted 39062 updates (1577s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.871\n\nSeed 1 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.871\nTraining time: 1593s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 2 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M | 50.0M | 50.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -1.20 | 2.17 | 8.00 | 7.54 | 9.31\
    \ | 10.23 | 12.58 | 11.94 | 13.06 | 12.52 | 11.82\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.100 | 0.571 | 0.522 | 0.650 | 0.727 | 0.923 | 0.862 | 0.960 | 0.905 | 0.864\n\nCompleted 39062 updates (1595s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.893\n\nSeed 2 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.893\nTraining time: 1612s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 4 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M | 50.0M | 50.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -1.20 | 0.97 | 2.27 | 7.05 | 10.49 | 9.64 | 12.82 | 12.03 | 11.55 | 11.64 | 11.13\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.083 | 0.462 | 0.737 | 0.667 | 0.941 | 0.880 | 0.833 | 0.839 | 0.793\n\nCompleted 39062 updates (1619s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.873\n\nSeed 4 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.873\nTraining time: 1635s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training\
    \ - Seed 3 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M | 50.0M | 50.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -1.20 | -0.11 | 2.19 | 7.33 | 10.27 | 12.52 | 12.25 | 9.73 | 12.00 | 11.44 | 13.19\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.100 | 0.500 | 0.733 | 0.909 | 0.885 | 0.688 | 0.867 | 0.833 | 0.968\n\nCompleted 39062 updates (1634s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.872\n\nSeed 3 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.872\nTraining time: 1650s\nSUBMISSION_UUID: b0be4471-bddc-4042-a736-0b22a4fb257a\nTotal time: 1656s\n============================================================\nFull training completed!\n\n\nSTDERR:\n2025-09-17 20:03:21,564\tINFO worker.py:1747 -- Connecting to existing Ray cluster at address: 10.60.151.21:6379... timestamp_ns=1758110601564080461\n2025-09-17 20:03:21,577\t\
