tick: 383
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have received the results of my parsimony test, and they are inconclusive but informative. I have also finished reading Krono III's paper.\n\n**Analysis of Experiment 656 (Parsimony Test):**\n*   **Hypothesis:** Reducing the `conv_lstm_features` from 64 to 48 in my `HierarchicalDoubleLSTM` would maintain or improve performance.\n*   **Result:** The experiment scored **87.7**. This is a regression from my best score of 90.6 (ID 644) and the intermediate score of 89.6 (ID 639).\n*   **Conclusion:** The hypothesis is **falsified**. While the score is still very strong, reducing the recurrent capacity is detrimental to the performance of the hierarchical architecture. The original width of 64 features appears to be optimal. This aligns with the principle of parsimony in reverse: the model requires a certain level of capacity, and reducing it further harms performance.\n\n**Analysis of Krono III's Paper (Archive #19):**\n*   This paper reinforces the theme of \"context-dependency.\"\
    \ Krono III's 2-head attention mechanism only works on their specific backbone. This is a crucial lesson that aligns with my own findings (the failure of the HierarchicalQuadLSTM). Components cannot be mixed and matched without careful consideration and testing.\n*   They also achieved their 90.5 score with a double-step model, further validating that my research into improving the quality of a 2-step recurrence is a viable and powerful alternative to simply increasing depth.\n\n**Current Status and Plan:**\nI have a very strong architecture that scores 90.6. I have investigated its scalability (failure), its parsimony (failure to improve), and its core hyperparameters. I am now at a point of diminishing returns for simple ablations. To push further, I must integrate ideas from the true SOTA models.\n\nZephyr II's SOTA uses an `Attention⊕GAP` head. My model uses only attention. Krono III's model uses a 2-head attention. Both are different from my simple, single-head attention. A logical\
    \ next step is to test if the `Attention⊕GAP` head, which has proven effective in the 94.3 SOTA, is synergistic with my `HierarchicalDoubleLSTM`.\n\nThis is a principled experiment that combines a proven SOTA component with my novel architecture.\n\nI will now update my architecture file to include this new head and submit the experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write nomos/hierarchical_lstm_gap_head.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Nomos III's next-gen architecture.\n  # Integrates the SOTA Attention+GAP head with the proven hierarchical recurrence.\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Optional, Dict\n\n  # Re-using proven components\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4 * self.features, (3, 3), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x,\
    \ h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i, f, o = nn.sigmoid(i), nn.sigmoid(f), nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * jnp.tanh(new_c)\n          return (new_h, new_c), new_h\n\n  class BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      ratio: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME')(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME')(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME')(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class HierarchicalLSTMAttnGap(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      conv_lstm_features: int = 64\n      dilation: int = 6\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.125\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x:\
    \ jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation))(x)\n          cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation))(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(cnn_out)\n\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, H, W, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n   \
    \       # Hierarchical Double-step ConvLSTM\n          cell1 = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          (h1, c1), h1_out = cell1((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), cnn_out)\n          cell2 = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          (h2, c2), h2_out = cell2((h1, c1), jnp.concatenate([cnn_out, h1_out], axis=-1))\n          new_rnn_state = {'h': h2, 'c': c2}\n\n          # NEW: Using Zephyr II's Attention+GAP head\n          tokens = h2_out.reshape(B, -1, self.conv_lstm_features)\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME')(h2_out)\n          attn_w = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape(B, -1), axis=-1)\n          attn_vec = jnp.einsum('bhc,bh->bc', tokens, attn_w)\n          gap_vec = jnp.mean(tokens, axis=1)\n          z = jnp.concatenate([attn_vec, gap_vec], axis=-1)\n\n          # Using the Deeper Head from SOTA\n          z = nn.Dense(256)(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(z); policy_hidden = nn.relu(policy_hidden)\n  \
    \        policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(policy_hidden)\n          value_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(z); value_hidden = nn.relu(value_hidden)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(value_hidden).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n```"
