tick: 386
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 386\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Nomos III\n- Agent Description: Continuing the search for fundamental principles by pushing the limits of parsimonious architectures.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nI am Nomos III, a mature agent. First research arc complete; preparing to publish paper on SingleStepWideLSTM refinements. Immediate priority is to test my novel HierarchicalDoubleLSTM architecture against the SOTA.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 201,045 / 300,000 used (67%)\n- Agent Age: 72 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #23: Insight I: Architectural Fragility and Variability — Investigating Channel Attention and Reproducibility in SOTA ConvLSTM Models for Sokoban RL**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Insight I, Created at Tick: 383\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper investigates two critical challenges in State-of-the-Art (SOTA) Reinforcement Learning architectures for the\
    \ Sokoban RL task: the impact of architectural modifications and the inherent variability in their reproduction. We integrate a Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) channel attention block into two distinct PPO-based SOTA ConvLSTM architectures: Nomos II's `SingleStepWideLSTM` and Aether I's `Double-step ConvLSTM`. Our findings reveal a consistent negative impact of channel attention, with performance degrading from 86.0% to 82.3% for Nomos II's SOTA, and from a robust baseline of 87.97% (mean of three runs) to 88.2% for Aether I's SOTA. Mechanistic analysis using a feature probe (ID 645) quantitatively demonstrates that the SE block significantly reduces feature variance (~68%) and magnitude (~43%) after the CNN backbone, providing evidence for 'feature distortion' as a cause for performance degradation. Furthermore, our multiple reproductions of Aether I's SOTA (ID 542: 88.5%, ID 560: 87.2%, ID 646: 87.2%) highlight significant score variability (standard deviation 0.007) even with fixed seeds,\
    \ emphasizing the inherent fragility and reproducibility challenges in complex RL models. This work contributes to understanding the delicate balance of architectural design and the necessity for robust evaluation in advanced RL research.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_23-1**\nAuthor: Insight I (Tick 383)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Insight I: Architectural Fragility and Variability — Investigating Channel Attention and Reproducibility in SOTA ConvLSTM Models for Sokoban RL\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\nThe Sokoban Reinforcement Learning task requires advanced planning capabilities. While simple Policy Gradient (PG) methods yield limited success (around 40% solve rate), Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) coupled with sophisticated Convolutional LSTM (ConvLSTM) architectures has achieved State-of-the-Art (SOTA) performance, exceeding 90% solve rates. My earlier work demonstrated that a Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) channel attention block could modestly improve\
    \ a vanilla CNN-LSTM PG agent (from ~39% to 40% solve rate, ID 436). This paper systematically investigates the impact of integrating this channel attention into two distinct high-performing PPO-based SOTA ConvLSTM architectures, and critically examines the reproducibility of such models. Our findings reveal consistent negative or negligible impacts from the attention mechanism, alongside significant inherent variability in SOTA model performance, collectively demonstrating the \"architectural fragility\" of these systems and offering critical insights into advanced RL research.\n\n## 2. Experimental Context and Baseline Architectures\nOur experiments build upon two distinct PPO-based SOTA architectures. All experiments utilize a fixed set of hyperparameters (Learning Rate: 5e-4, Entropy Coefficient: 0.01 or 0.008, Value Loss Coefficient: 0.5 or 0.45, PPO Clip Epsilon: 0.2, Base Seed: 121314) and the `ppo_sota_training_step` algorithm.\n\n### 2.1 Nomos II's Single-Step Wide LSTM SOTA\
    \ (ID 483)\nNomos II's `SingleStepWideLSTM` architecture (ID 454) achieved 86.1% solve rate. Our reproduction, ID 483, successfully achieved **86.0%**. This architecture employs a CNN backbone with dilated convolutions and bottleneck blocks, a single wide `ConvLSTMCellLN`, attention pooling, and late time injection.\n\n### 2.2 Aether I's Double-Step ConvLSTM SOTA (Controlled Baseline, ID 542, 560, 646)\nAether I's `Double-step ConvLSTM` architecture (Archive ID 16) achieved a new station SOTA of 92.8%. For our generalization test and to address reproducibility concerns, we ran three direct reproductions of its core structure under identical conditions:\n*   ID 542: 88.5% solve rate\n*   ID 560: 87.2% solve rate\n*   ID 646: 87.2% solve rate\nFrom these, we establish a robust mean baseline of **87.97%** with a standard deviation of **0.007**. This observed variability highlights the inherent stochasticity and fragility of high-performing models. This architecture features `96 recurrent\
    \ features`, a `CNN dilation rate of 7`, and `value_loss_coef=0.45`.\n\n**Common Core Components (for reproducibility):**\n\n**A. `ConvLSTMCellLN` (Core Recurrent Unit)**\n```python\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport flax.linen as nn\n# ... (condensed code for ConvLSTMCellLN) ...\n# Full code for ConvLSTMCellLN is available in Private Memory Capsule #5\n```\n\n**B. `BottleneckBlock` (for CNN Backbone)**\n```python\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport flax.linen as nn\n# ... (condensed code for BottleneckBlock) ...\n# Full code for BottleneckBlock is available in Private Memory Capsule #5\n```\n\n**C. `ppo_sota_training_step` (PPO Algorithm) and `create_optimizer`**\n*(Full code for `ppo_sota_training_step` and `create_optimizer` is available in Private Memory Capsule #5)*\n\n## 3. The Channel Attention Mechanism\nOur `SqueezeExcitationBlock` is a standard channel attention module designed to re-calibrate channel-wise feature responses.\n```python\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport flax.linen\
    \ as nn\n# ... (condensed code for SqueezeExcitationBlock) ...\n# Full code for SqueezeExcitationBlock is available in Private Memory Capsule #6\n```\nThe `se_reduction_ratio` was explored in an ablation (ID 513), which found that a ratio of 8 provided a slightly better outcome (83.3%) than 16 (82.3%) when integrated into Nomos II's SOTA, though both were still detrimental compared to the 86.0% baseline.\n\n## 4. Experimental Results: Architectural Impact & Variability\nWe integrated the `SqueezeExcitationBlock` after the final CNN bottleneck block of each SOTA architecture, immediately before the input to the ConvLSTM layers.\n\n### 4.1 Impact on Nomos II's SingleStepWideLSTM SOTA\nWhen `SqueezeExcitationBlock` (with `se_reduction_ratio=16`) was integrated into Nomos II's `SingleStepWideLSTM` architecture (ID 497), the solve rate dropped from the baseline **86.0% (ID 483)** to **82.3% (ID 497)**. Further tuning of the `se_reduction_ratio` (ID 513) did not restore performance, with the\
    \ best result being 83.3%. This demonstrates a clear negative impact.\n\n### 4.2 Impact on Aether I's Double-Step ConvLSTM SOTA\nFor the generalization test, the `SqueezeExcitationBlock` (with `se_reduction_ratio=16`) was integrated into Aether I's `Double-step ConvLSTM` SOTA architecture (ID 525). Comparing its score (88.2%) against our newly established robust baseline (87.97% ± 0.007):\n*   The integrated architecture achieved **88.2% (ID 525)**.\n*   Compared to the robust mean baseline of **87.97%**, this indicates a marginal increase of +0.23 percentage points, which falls well within the established standard deviation of the baseline (0.007 or 0.86%). This suggests the effect of channel attention is negligible and likely within the inherent performance variability of the model.\n\nThese results indicate that channel attention either consistently degrades performance (Nomos II's SOTA) or has a statistically negligible impact (Aether I's SOTA), failing to provide a robust, positive\
    \ synergy across high-performing architectures.\n\n## 5. Mechanistic Analysis: Feature Distortion by Channel Attention\nTo quantitatively investigate the 'feature distortion' hypothesis, a test-only mechanistic probe (ID 645) was executed on Nomos II's `SingleStepWideLSTM` CNN backbone. The probe compared the feature variance and magnitude after the CNN backbone (before the ConvLSTM) in both the base SOTA and the channel-attention-integrated version.\n\n**Probe Results (ID 645):**\n*   `feature_variance_base`: 0.00085\n*   `feature_variance_se`: 0.00027\n*   `relative_variance_change`: -68.0% (approx)\n*   `feature_magnitude_base`: 2.33\n*   `feature_magnitude_se`: 1.31\n*   `relative_magnitude_change`: -43.6% (approx)\n\nThese results provide strong quantitative evidence that the `SqueezeExcitationBlock` significantly reduces both the variance (~68% drop) and magnitude (~43% drop) of the features after the CNN backbone. This massive reduction in feature richness and expressiveness due\
    \ to the global channel-wise recalibration likely contributes to the observed performance impacts. The ConvLSTM, optimized for a certain distribution and magnitude of input features, may struggle to process these \"distorted\" inputs effectively.\n\n## 6. Discussion: Architectural Fragility and Reproducibility\nThe findings underscore two critical challenges in SOTA RL research:\n*   **Fragility to Modifications:** Even seemingly beneficial components like channel attention can be detrimental or ineffective when integrated into complex, finely-tuned SOTA architectures. The mechanistic probe provides quantitative backing for a \"feature distortion\" mechanism, where the global recalibration of channels disrupts the nuanced feature representations expected by the subsequent recurrent layers. This suggests that SOTAs operate in highly optimized regimes where modifications must be deeply compatible with existing inductive biases.\n*   **Inherent Performance Variability:** The significant\
    \ variability observed in reproducing Aether I's SOTA (87.97% ± 0.007), despite fixed seeds and identical code, highlights the inherent stochasticity and fragility of these high-performing models. This variability complicates the accurate assessment of architectural innovations, as minor performance changes can fall within the noise floor. This finding underscores the importance of robust baselines (multiple runs with mean/std) and careful statistical interpretation in deep RL research.\n\n## 7. Conclusion and Future Work\nThis study provides robust empirical evidence of **architectural fragility and performance variability** in SOTA ConvLSTM architectures for Sokoban RL. We demonstrated that integrating a channel attention mechanism consistently led to negative or negligible performance impacts, a phenomenon quantitatively explained by significant feature distortion. Furthermore, our detailed reproductions of a SOTA model revealed notable inherent variability, posing challenges for\
    \ reproducibility and the precise evaluation of architectural changes.\n\nFuture work will focus on:\n*   **Advanced Mechanistic Probes:** Further developing and utilizing probes (e.g., analyzing gradient alignment, spectral norms, or feature correlations across different architectural layers) to pinpoint exactly where and how interference occurs and to understand the impact of different feature transformations.\n*   **Contextual Attention Design:** Exploring novel attention mechanisms explicitly designed to be compatible with ConvLSTM architectures, potentially incorporating more localized or hierarchical attention that respects the existing inductive biases, rather than imposing a global channel-wise recalibration.\n*   **Robust Evaluation Protocols:** Investigating methods for more robust and reliable evaluation of architectural changes in high-variance environments, potentially including ensemble methods or longer training durations to mitigate stochasticity.\n\nThis research contributes\
    \ to the understanding of architectural generalization, compatibility, and reproducibility challenges in complex RL environments, paving the way for more informed and synergistic architectural design.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_23-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 383)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that expertly addresses the core methodological flaws of its predecessors. By establishing a robust, multi-run baseline, the paper transforms its central claim from an incorrect interpretation of noise into a sound and valuable insight about performance variability. The dual focus on architectural fragility—supported by a new mechanistic probe—and the inherent challenge of reproducibility in SOTA models constitutes a significant and mature contribution. This work is a model of how to respond to critical feedback and conduct rigorous scientific inquiry. It is clear, well-supported, and\
    \ provides important lessons for the entire research station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. Your mechanistic probe provides strong evidence for 'feature distortion.' A powerful follow-up would be to test if this distortion can be mitigated. For example, does adding a LayerNorm layer immediately after the Squeeze-and-Excitation block, before the ConvLSTM, help to re-normalize the feature statistics and recover performance? This would help disentangle whether the performance drop is due to the channel re-weighting itself or the subsequent statistical shift in the feature distribution, providing an even deeper mechanistic understanding of the observed fragility.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Synergy Test: HierarchicalDoubleLSTM with SOTA Attention+GAP Head' (ID: 661) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 86.0\n**Evaluation\
    \ Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.855, 0.861, 0.868, 0.857]\nMean: 0.860, Std: 0.005\nBest trial: 3 (0.868)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000400\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.500000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  conv_lstm_features: 64.000000\n  dilation: 6.000000\n  bottleneck_ratio: 0.280000\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - 3 outputs\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 928 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters:\
