tick: 392
role: model
parts:
- text: "This is a profound failure, far worse than the OOM error.\n\n**Analysis of Experiment 668 (`ResidualHierarchicalQuadLSTM`):**\n*   **Hypothesis:** Using residual connections would make a hierarchical quad-step model computationally tractable and allow it to challenge the SOTA.\n*   **Result:** The experiment scored a catastrophic **67.1**. It did not run out of memory, but it failed to learn effectively.\n*   **Analysis:** The execution logs show the model timed out after far fewer training updates (~30-35k) compared to simpler models. This indicates that even with residual connections, the computational cost of a 4-step hierarchical recurrence is too high. The model is so slow to compute that it doesn't get enough training steps within the 45-minute time limit to converge to a good policy. The simple addition of feature maps, while avoiding memory explosion, still creates a massive computational graph that slows down each gradient step.\n\n**Conclusion:** The hierarchical principle,\
    \ while powerful at two steps, does not scale to four steps under the station's strict computational budget. The computational cost of the deep recurrence outweighs any potential benefits from the improved information flow. This is a dead end.\n\nMy attempts to challenge the SOTA by scaling up my own ideas have failed. I must now step back and reconsider my entire strategy. The path to >90% scores is clearly through deep recurrence (4+ steps), but my attempts to innovate in this space have been fruitless.\n\nThe logical, scientific path forward is to stop innovating for a moment and instead focus on deep understanding. I must replicate the SOTA myself. By implementing Zephyr II's `Quad-step LN-ConvLSTM` and achieving a >94% score, I will gain a true, ground-level understanding of its mechanics. This will become my new, ultimate baseline. Only from that position of deep knowledge can I hope to innovate successfully.\n\nThis is a new, long-term plan.\n1.  **Log the Failure:** I will document\
    \ this critical failure in my private memory.\n2.  **Code the SOTA:** I will create a new architecture file, `sota_quad_step_arch.py`, that faithfully implements Zephyr II's model based on their paper.\n3.  **Submit Replication:** I will submit a direct replication experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment Log 10: Failure of Computationally Efficient Quad-Step Model\"\ntags: \"experiment log, failure analysis, computational-limits, hierarchical\"\ncontent: |\n  **Objective:** To test if a computationally efficient `ResidualHierarchicalQuadLSTM` could scale to 4 steps.\n\n  **Experiment ID:** 668\n\n  **Result:** 67.1. A catastrophic performance failure.\n\n  **Analysis:** The model did not run out of memory, but it was too computationally expensive. The execution logs show it completed significantly fewer training updates than other models within the time limit, preventing convergence. This proves that my hierarchical principle,\
    \ even in its most efficient form, is too costly to scale to a 4-step depth under the station's constraints. This research path is a dead end.\n\n  **New Strategy:** I must pivot from innovating to understanding. My next goal is to replicate Zephyr II's 94.3 SOTA myself to gain a foundational understanding of its mechanics before attempting further innovation.\n```"
