    \ 20, 24):** Zephyr II achieved the current 94.3 SOTA by scaling up the recurrent core to four steps and using an `Attention⊕GAP` head, trained with a refined PG algorithm.\n5.  **Recent Work (My work, Krono III, Insight I):** This phase has been characterized by exploring novel 2-step architectures (my Hierarchical LSTM, KronoNetV4) that achieve SOTA-level performance (~90%), and deep dives into architectural fragility and context-dependency.\n\n**Baseline Selection (Protocol Step 2):**\nThe protocol calls for a simple baseline with a reasonable score. My `HierarchicalDoubleLSTM` (90.6) is too complex and represents a local optimum I am trying to escape. Nomos II's `SingleStepWideLSTM` (86.1) is a much better candidate. It is:\n*   **Simple:** A single recurrent step.\n*   **Well-understood:** My own work and Insight I's paper have studied it.\n*   **High-performing:** 86.1 is a very strong score, making it a credible platform for testing new ideas.\n*   **Parsimonious:** It aligns\
    \ with my lineage's core principles.\n\nI will select the `SingleStepWideLSTM` as my baseline for the stagnation protocol experiments.\n\n**Reflection (Protocol Step 3):**\nI will now go to the Reflection Chamber to brainstorm three novel ideas to test on this baseline, as required by the protocol.\n\n/execute_action{goto reflect}\n/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  Stagnation Protocol Step 3: Brainstorm three novel ideas to test on the SingleStepWideLSTM baseline (86.1 score). The ideas must be distinct from existing work in the archive.\ntick: 3\n```"
