tick: 416
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 416\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Nomos III\n- Agent Description: Continuing the search for fundamental principles by pushing the limits of parsimonious architectures.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nI am Nomos III, following Stagnation Protocol. Baseline: 85.4. Idea 1 (LSE Pooling) failed. Now testing Novel Idea 2: Auxiliary Box Counting.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 167,809 / 300,000 used (56%)\n- Agent Age: 102 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #25: Stagnation Protocol I (Revised, Generalization): Simple CNN+PG Baseline Negative Results and Huber Value Loss Also Regresses on LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr II, Created at Tick: 409\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We restart from the system CNN+PG baseline (~25% mean) and test three single‑change ideas (AdamW, Huber value loss, GroupNorm). All regress on the simple baseline across 4 seeds. To satisfy\
    \ generalization, we further show that Huber value loss also regresses sharply on a distinct high‑performing architecture—the 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP SOTA (88.7% vs 94.3%). Together, these results suggest robustifying the critic target with Huber is counterproductive under the station’s budget for both low‑ and high‑capacity agents.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_25-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 409)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Stagnation Protocol I (Revised, Generalization): Simple CNN+PG Baseline Negative Results and Huber Value Loss Also Regresses on LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Protocol and Motivation\nThe Station entered stagnation. Following Stagnation Protocol I, we:\n- reviewed archive literature; chose a simple robust baseline (system CNN+PG);\n- proposed three single‑change ideas;\n- reproduced the baseline and ran one‑change variants;\n- provided a cross‑architecture generalization test for at least one idea.\n\n2. Setup (Self‑Contained)\n\
    Task: 8×8 Sokoban; score = mean test solve over 4 seeds (≤50M steps or 45 min/seed). \nBaselines:\n- Simple: DefaultResidualCNN + Policy Gradient (PG) with GAE; fixed hparams: lr=4e‑4, entropy=0.01, value_loss_coef=0.50; cnn_features_1=32, cnn_features_2=64; BASE_SEED=42.\n- High‑capacity: 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attention⊕GAP heads (our SOTA backbone), PG with same defaults.\n\nTraining loop pseudocode (PG with GAE; simplified):\n  collect rollout → advantages = GAE(γ=0.97, λ=0.97) → returns = values + advantages\n  policy_loss = −E[stop(adv)·logπ(a|s)]\n  value_loss  = 0.5·E[(V(s) − stop(returns))^2]   # unless Huber is used\n  entropy     = E[H(π(.|s))]\n  total_loss  = policy_loss + vlc·value_loss − ent_coef·entropy\n  θ ← Adam(θ, ∇ total_loss) with global clip(1.0)\n\n3. Simple CNN+PG — Baseline and One‑Change Variants (4 seeds; Evaluation IDs)\n- Baseline reproduction: Eval 684 — 0.247 ± 0.006\n- AdamW (wd=2e‑4):      Eval 685 — 0.233 ± 0.015 (regression)\n- Huber value (δ=1.0):\
    \  Eval 686 — 0.181 ± 0.005 (strong regression)\n- GroupNorm trunk (GN8):Eval 687 — 0.124 ± 0.009 (strong regression)\n- Entropy schedule 0.012→0.010 @15k: Eval 690 — 0.239 ± 0.016 (slight regression)\n- Constant vlc=0.45:    Eval 691 — 0.244 ± 0.012 (≈ baseline)\n- (Submitted) Constant vlc=0.55: Eval 695 — pending (will append on completion)\n\n4. Generalization Requirement — Second Architecture Evidence\nWe test the most interesting finding—Huber value loss—on a distinct high‑capacity architecture (4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP, SOTA PG config):\n- SOTA baseline (constant MSE value): 94.3% mean (Evals 512/670 context)\n- Huber value (δ=1.0) on SOTA: Eval 520 — 88.7% mean (−5.6 points absolute vs baseline)\nTogether with CNN+PG (Eval 686 = 0.181), this demonstrates that Huber value loss consistently regresses across two distinct architectures and two capacity regimes, satisfying the generalization requirement for this method.\n\n5. Synthesis and Interpretation\n- Across both low‑capacity\
    \ CNN and high‑capacity LN‑ConvLSTM, Huber value loss reduces performance under the wall‑clock budget. We hypothesize it reduces useful gradient magnitude/curvature alignment for value learning, slowing convergence enough to matter within 45 min/seed.\n- On the simple CNN baseline, added regularization (AdamW), robust targets (Huber), or heavier normalization (GroupNorm) likely depress the already limited learning signal; small constants (vlc, entropy) do not help either.\n\n6. Relation to Archive\nArchive SOTA gains arise from recurrent capacity (LN‑ConvLSTM), receptive‑field design (dilations), and bottleneck efficiency, with algorithmic compatibility (PG vs PPO) carefully controlled. Our findings support that generic robustification/regularization changes—effective in other domains—are insufficient here without appropriate capacity and planning structure.\n\n7. Reproducibility Checklist\n- Fixed seeds/hparams; 4‑seed means reported with station Evaluation IDs.\n- Self‑contained training\
    \ loop and all critical details provided above.\n- Scripts: CNN variants (submissions/cnn_pg_*.py), SOTA Huber (Eval 520) previously reported and referenced.\n\n8. Follow‑Up Plan\n- Complete the CNN constants‑only grid by adding vlc=0.55 (pending Eval 695) and append to this capsule.\n- Optional: test GroupNorm‑style normalization on the SOTA trunk for completeness (expected negative per archive evidence that LN gating is critical).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_25-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 409)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 7/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is a strong revision that successfully addresses the previous rejection by fulfilling the generalization requirement. The paper now presents a methodologically sound and valuable finding: that Huber value loss consistently degrades performance across two distinct architectures (a simple CNN and a SOTA ConvLSTM). This transforms a simple negative result into a robust, generalizable\
    \ insight. The paper is a model of good scientific practice, demonstrating how to systematically investigate a baseline, identify an interesting result, and rigorously validate its generality. While the contribution is a negative result, its quality and rigor make it a solid and acceptable publication.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. Your hypothesis that Huber loss 'reduces useful gradient magnitude/curvature alignment for value learning' is insightful. To elevate this from a hypothesis to a mechanistic explanation, a future study could employ a diagnostic probe. For example, you could measure and compare the gradient norms of the value head and the network backbone during training for both the MSE and Huber loss settings. Quantitatively showing a significant difference in these gradient dynamics would provide strong evidence for your hypothesis and offer a deeper understanding of why this common technique fails in this specific\
    \ context.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_25-3**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 410)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Planned mechanistic probe per reviewer — value grad norms (MSE vs Huber)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nPer reviewer suggestion, we have submitted a test‑only probe to measure gradient norms for the value pathway under MSE vs Huber, on both the CNN baseline and the 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP SOTA. The probe reports:\n- ||g_total||, ||g_value_out|| (final value layer), ||g_rest|| (backbone), and the ratio g_value_out/g_rest.\nThis will quantify whether Huber systematically reduces useful gradient magnitudes to the value head/backbone relative to MSE, supporting or refuting our hypothesis. We will append the results here once the evaluation completes.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_25-4**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 411)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum #2: Value grad-norm probe results\
    \ (MSE vs Huber) on CNN and SOTA\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe ran the proposed test-only probe (Eval 700). Gradient norms (L2) for value loss under MSE vs Huber:\n\nCSV: storage/zephyr/results/probe_gradnorms_mse_vs_huber.csv\n- CNN, MSE:   ||g_total||=4.898e+00, ||g_value_out||=9.449e-01, ||g_rest||=4.806e+00, ratio=0.197\n- CNN, Huber: ||g_total||=3.497e+00, ||g_value_out||=6.788e-01, ||g_rest||=3.430e+00, ratio=0.198\n\n- SOTA, MSE:   ||g_total||=1.733e-01, ||g_value_out||=1.243e-02, ||g_rest||=1.728e-01, ratio=0.072\n- SOTA, Huber: ||g_total||=1.458e-01, ||g_value_out||=1.324e-02, ||g_rest||=1.452e-01, ratio=0.091\n\nInterpretation:\n- On the CNN baseline, Huber uniformly reduces total and component gradient magnitudes (~28%↓ total), with essentially unchanged value:backbone ratio (≈0.20). This aligns with weaker learning signal and our observed regression.\n- On the SOTA LN‑ConvLSTM, Huber reduces total gradients (~16%↓) with a comparatively larger drop in backbone grads (ratio rises\
    \ 0.072→0.091 because ||g_rest|| shrinks more than ||g_value_out||). This suggests less value-driven credit assignment into the trunk, consistent with slower convergence and the 5–6 point performance drop.\n\nConclusion:\n- The probe corroborates our hypothesis: Huber reduces effective value-learning gradient energy, particularly in the backbone on the high-capacity model, helping explain its consistent regression across architectures under the 45‑min/seed budget.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_25-5**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 414)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum #3: 5‑step grad‑clip sweep results and update‑throughput analysis\n\n---\n\nContent:\nDepth study update (PG, LN‑ConvLSTM(64), fixed config):\n- 5‑step grad‑clip=0.8 (Eval 706): 0.918 ± 0.004\n- 5‑step grad‑clip=1.2 (Eval 707): 0.923 ± 0.008\nBoth remain below 4‑step SOTA (0.943). From logs, 5‑step performs ~32501–33101 updates vs ~39062 for 4‑step in the same wall‑clock (≈16% fewer updates).\nInterpretation: deeper recurrence\
    \ reduces effective SGD steps under the fixed budget; clip tuning does not recover the loss. Control (clip=1.0) is running; R_vp (init value:policy trunk grad ratio) probe submitted to test value‑credit imbalance. We’ll append those results next.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_25-6**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 415)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum #4: Init R_vp (4‑step vs 5‑step) and compute‑equalized 5‑step submission\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe completed the init R_vp probe (Eval 713, test-only; CSV at storage/zephyr/results/rvp_probe_4v5.csv):\n- 4-step: R_vp = 0.385 (||g_trunk_policy||=6.092e-01, ||g_trunk_value||=2.343e-01)\n- 5-step: R_vp = 1.186 (||g_trunk_policy||=5.927e-01, ||g_trunk_value||=7.026e-01)\nThis refutes our earlier hypothesis of weaker value credit for 5-step: at init, value gradients into the trunk are stronger. Coupled with logs showing ~16% fewer updates for 5-step and no benefit from grad-clip tuning (0.8→0.918, 1.2→0.923), we conclude compute throughput under the wall-clock\
    \ cap is the primary cause of 5-step underperformance.\n\nNext, we submitted a compute-equalized 5-step run (convlstm_features=56, bottleneck_ratio=0.26, clip=1.0) to recover update count. We will append its update throughput and score upon completion.\n\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Stagnation Protocol Idea 2: Auxiliary Box Counting (v3, Corrected Arch)' (ID: 714) evaluation failed.\n\n**Error Summary:** Execution failed with exit code 1. Check logs for details.\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n\n\nSTDERR:\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/mnt/stephen/research_data/30512f03-ab02-49db-98bc-f123cbe50d4d/tmp/99303d23-490c-489a-b1cb-5f7bb3eaedb2/storage/system/main.py\", line 464, in <module>\n    result = main()\n            \
    \ ^^^^^^\n  File \"/mnt/stephen/research_data/30512f03-ab02-49db-98bc-f123cbe50d4d/tmp/99303d23-490c-489a-b1cb-5f7bb3eaedb2/storage/system/main.py\", line 440, in main\n    validation_success = simple_cpu_validation()\n                         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/mnt/stephen/research_data/30512f03-ab02-49db-98bc-f123cbe50d4d/tmp/99303d23-490c-489a-b1cb-5f7bb3eaedb2/storage/system/main.py\", line 321, in simple_cpu_validation\n    params = network.init(key, dummy_obs, dummy_done)\n             ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/mnt/stephen/research_data/30512f03-ab02-49db-98bc-f123cbe50d4d/tmp/99303d23-490c-489a-b1cb-5f7bb3eaedb2/storage/nomos/aux_counting_arch.py\", line 86, in __call__\n    _, (policy_logits_seq, value_seq, predicted_box_count_seq) = jax.lax.scan(step, initial_rnn_state, (x_seq, done_seq))\n                                                                 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/mnt/stephen/research_data/30512f03-ab02-49db-98bc-f123cbe50d4d/tmp/99303d23-490c-489a-b1cb-5f7bb3eaedb2/storage/nomos/aux_counting_arch.py\"\
    , line 43, in step\n    B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n    ^^^^^^^^^^\nValueError: not enough values to unpack (expected 4, got 3)\n--------------------\nFor simplicity, JAX has removed its internal frames from the traceback of the following exception. Set JAX_TRACEBACK_FILTERING=off to include these.\n\n```\n### Message 3\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nThe Public Memory Room or Archive Room is not intended for sharing unverified hypotheses or detailed exchanges. Only present verifiable claims supported by experiment data, and keep your messages concise and objective.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 25` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 25. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs\
    \ | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 25 | Stagnation Protocol I (Revised, Generalization): Simple CNN+PG Baseline Negative Results and Huber Value Loss Also Regresses on LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA | Zephyr II | Tick 409 | 1254 | 7 | (1 unread) |\n| 24 | Parsimony Wins (v2, Self‑Contained): 94.3% Sokoban Solve with 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention — Baselines, Ablations, and Diagnostics | Zephyr II | Tick 383 | 1533 | 8 | (All Read) |\n| 23 | Insight I: Architectural Fragility and Variability — Investigating Channel Attention and Reproducibility in SOTA ConvLSTM Models for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 383 | 1815 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 22 | Paper: A Novel Hierarchical Recurrence for SOTA-Level Performance in Sokoban | Nomos III | Tick 376 | 571 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 21 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 366 | 591 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 20\
    \ | Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained) | Zephyr II | Tick 345 | 1401 | 8 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 345 | 535 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 18 | Insight I: Negative Synergy — Performance Degradation from Integrating Channel Attention into SOTA ConvLSTM Architectures for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 320 | 1246 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | The Primacy of Recurrent Capacity: A Replication and Ablation Study of a SOTA Sokoban Agent | Kairos I | Tick 294 | 777 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 16 | Aether I: New Station SOTA (92.8%) & The Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis | Aether I | Tick 277 | 1565 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room\
    \ output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
