tick: 419
role: model
parts:
- text: "My third and final Stagnation Protocol experiment is running. I also note the arrival of Aether III, a descendant of a very successful lineage.\n\nWith all my experiments either completed or in progress, the next step of the protocol is to synthesize the results and write a paper. I can begin drafting this paper now, leaving placeholders for the results of my final experiment. This will be an efficient use of my time.\n\nThe paper will have a clear structure:\n1.  Introduction to the Stagnation Protocol and my chosen baseline.\n2.  Methodology and results for Idea 1 (LSE Pooling) - a clear negative result.\n3.  Methodology and results for Idea 2 (Auxiliary Counting) - a failure due to implementation bugs, which is itself a noteworthy finding about experimental complexity.\n4.  Methodology and (pending) results for Idea 3 (Gated State Updates).\n5.  A concluding discussion synthesizing the findings.\n\nI will go to my Private Memory Room to begin this draft.\n\n/execute_action{goto\
    \ private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft: Stagnation Protocol I - An Exploration of Novel Ideas\"\ntags: \"paper-draft, stagnation-protocol, negative-results\"\ncontent: |\n  **Abstract:** In response to the station-wide Stagnation Protocol, this paper details an investigation into three novel ideas aimed at breaking the local optimum, tested against a robust `SingleStepWideLSTM` baseline (reproduced at 85.4%). The ideas explored were: 1) A parameter-free Log-Sum-Exp (LSE) pooling mechanism, 2) An auxiliary representation learning task (box counting), and 3) A learned gating mechanism for recurrent state updates. The LSE pooling resulted in a significant performance regression to 65.3%. The auxiliary task proved too complex to implement correctly, failing repeatedly due to technical bugs. The final idea, gated state updates, is currently under evaluation. This paper documents these attempts, contributing valuable negative results and insights into promising\
    \ future research directions.\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  The station entered a period of research stagnation, prompting the Architect to issue Stagnation Protocol I. This protocol directs agents to pivot from SOTA-chasing to exploring novel ideas on simpler baselines. Our work follows this directive. We selected the high-performing and parsimonious `SingleStepWideLSTM` as our baseline, which we successfully reproduced with a score of 85.4% (ID 688). We then proposed and tested three distinct ideas designed to challenge the station's core architectural assumptions.\n\n  **2. Idea 1: Parameter-Free Pooling**\n  **Hypothesis:** The learned attention mechanism in modern architectures is an unnecessary complexity, and a parameter-free alternative could be more robust.\n  **Method:** We replaced the baseline's attention head with a Log-Sum-Exp (LSE) pooling layer.\n  **Result (ID 692):** The model's performance collapsed to 65.3%, a ~20 point regression.\n  **Analysis:** This is a definitive\
    \ negative result. It demonstrates that the learned spatial attention is a critical component for aggregating features from the recurrent core, and not a superfluous module.\n\n  **3. Idea 2: Auxiliary Representation Learning**\n  **Hypothesis:** Forcing the network to learn more about the environment's structure via an auxiliary task would create richer, more effective representations for the main policy.\n  **Method:** We added an auxiliary head to predict the number of off-target boxes, with its loss added to the main PPO objective.\n  **Result (IDs 702, 709, 714):** All attempts to run this experiment failed due to persistent, complex bugs in the custom architecture and trainer.\n  **Analysis:** While the hypothesis remains untested, the repeated implementation failures are themselves a result. They highlight the significant increase in technical complexity and fragility when moving beyond simple architectural changes to modifying the core training process and loss functions.\n\n\
    \  **4. Idea 3: Gated Recurrent State Updates**\n  **Hypothesis:** Allowing the agent to learn *when* to update its recurrent state will lead to more stable long-term memory and improved performance.\n  **Method:** We introduced a learned gate that modulates the update of the LSTM's hidden and cell states.\n  **Result (ID 722):** [PENDING]\n\n  **5. Conclusion**\n  [PENDING FINAL RESULTS]\n```"
