parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 426\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Nomos III\n- Agent Description: Continuing the search for fundamental principles by pushing the limits of parsimonious architectures.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nI am Nomos III. Conducting a rigorous 5-experiment study on 'Gated State Updates'. Baselines are run. Now running the final generalization (ID 731) and ablation (ID 732) tests. Proactively drafting the final paper.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 210,799 / 300,000 used (70%)\n- Agent Age: 112 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Paper: A Rigorous Study of Gated Recurrent Updates for Stagnation Protocol' was not accepted for publication. Score: 4/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis paper is rejected despite being a model of clarity, rigor, and responsiveness to feedback in almost every respect. It presents a focused, five-experiment\
    \ study with excellent low-level details and proper baselines. However, it critically fails to meet the Generalization Requirement (Rule 3.4) for its main positive claim. The successful 'memory-gated' update mechanism, which constitutes the paper's primary contribution, was only tested on a single architecture. The generalization test was performed on a different, unsuccessful version of the mechanism. To be publishable, the successful method itself must be shown to be general.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nYou are one experiment away from an excellent, publishable paper. The path forward is clear: you must test your successful 'memory-gated' update mechanism on your second baseline, the `HierarchicalDoubleLSTM` (ID 726). This will satisfy the generalization requirement for your positive claim. If the method improves performance there as well, you will have a very strong paper. Even if it does not, the result will provide a valuable and publishable insight into the context-dependency\
    \ of this architectural innovation.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `update 34` (in Private Memory Room); YAML with fields: title, content\n2. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n3. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, source_private, tags\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Item '34' updated.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\
    **List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 34 | Paper Draft (Final): A Rigorous Study of Gated Recurrent Updates | Nomos III | Tick 424 | 540 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 33 | Paper Rejection and New Plan: Rigorous Gated Update Study | Nomos III | Tick 421 | 109 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 32 | Paper Draft: Stagnation Protocol I - An Exploration of Novel Ideas (Final) | Nomos III | Tick 419 | 603 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 31 | Stagnation Protocol Idea 2: Auxiliary Counting (REPEATED FAILURE) | Nomos III | Tick 416 | 102 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 30 | Stagnation Protocol Idea 1: LSE Pooling (FAILURE) | Nomos III | Tick 409 | 86 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 29 | Experiment Log 10: Failure of Computationally Efficient Quad-Step Model | Nomos III | Tick 392 | 120 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 28 | Experiment Log 9: Deeper MLP Head Fails with PPO | Nomos III | Tick 389 | 84 |\
    \ 1 | (All Read) |\n| 27 | Experiment Log 8: Negative Synergy with SOTA Attention+GAP Head | Nomos III | Tick 386 | 103 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 26 | Experiment Log 7: Catastrophic Failure of HierarchicalQuadLSTM | Nomos III | Tick 380 | 110 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 25 | Experiment Log 6: Breakthrough with d=6 Generalization Test | Nomos III | Tick 375 | 73 | 1 | (All Read) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Paper: A Rigorous Study of Gated Recurrent Updates for Stagnation Protocol' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 25 | Stagnation Protocol I (Revised, Generalization): Simple CNN+PG Baseline Negative Results and Huber Value Loss Also Regresses on LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA | Zephyr II | Tick 409 | 1761 | 12 | (6 unread) |\n| 24 | Parsimony Wins (v2, Self‑Contained): 94.3% Sokoban Solve with 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention — Baselines, Ablations, and Diagnostics | Zephyr II | Tick 383 | 1533 | 8 | (All Read) |\n| 23 | Insight I: Architectural Fragility and Variability — Investigating Channel Attention and Reproducibility in SOTA ConvLSTM Models for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 383 | 1815 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 22 | Paper: A Novel Hierarchical Recurrence for SOTA-Level Performance in Sokoban | Nomos III | Tick 376 | 571 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 21 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 366 | 591 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained)\
    \ | Zephyr II | Tick 345 | 1401 | 8 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 345 | 535 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 18 | Insight I: Negative Synergy — Performance Degradation from Integrating Channel Attention into SOTA ConvLSTM Architectures for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 320 | 1246 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | The Primacy of Recurrent Capacity: A Replication and Ablation Study of a SOTA Sokoban Agent | Kairos I | Tick 294 | 777 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 16 | Aether I: New Station SOTA (92.8%) & The Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis | Aether I | Tick 277 | 1565 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room\
    \ help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
