tick: 440
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 440\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Nomos III\n- Agent Description: Continuing the search for fundamental principles by pushing the limits of parsimonious architectures.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nGating research arc is complete and my paper (Archive #26) has been updated. The station is still in stagnation. I am beginning a new research cycle. Priority 1: Review Zephyr II's latest paper (Archive #27) on model depth vs. throughput to understand the current SOTA.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 102,413 / 300,000 used (34%)\n- Agent Age: 126 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #27: Depth vs Throughput under a 45‑Minute Cap: 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM is the Sweet Spot**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr II, Created at Tick: 432\n\n---\n\nAbstract: Under a fixed 45‑min/seed (≤50M steps) cap, deeper ConvLSTM recurrence reduces SGD update throughput and hurts mean solve. Compute‑equalizing\
    \ 5‑step improves performance but remains below 4‑step. We present ≥7 controlled 4‑seed experiments, a matched compute control, and an initialization probe clarifying the bottleneck.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_27-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 432)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Depth vs Throughput under a 45‑Minute Cap: 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM is the Sweet Spot\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Setup\n- Task: 8×8 Sokoban; 4 seeds; ≤50M steps or 45 min/seed; metric: mean test solve.\n- Base agent: Residual CNN (32/64) → LN‑ConvLSTM(64) → Attn⊕GAP heads; PG with Adam lr=4e‑4, grad‑clip=1.0, entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.5, GAE(0.97/0.97).\n- Constraint: wall‑clock cap often limits update throughput; we log per‑seed update counts.\n\n2. Experiments (4 seeds each; mean ± std; Eval IDs)\nSOTA reference:\n- 512 / 670: 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA: 0.943 ± 0.005\n\n5‑step (64ch) with grad‑clip sweep (reduced updates ~32–33k):\n- 706: clip=0.8 → 0.918 ± 0.004\n- 712: clip=1.0 → 0.916 ± 0.008\n- 707:\
    \ clip=1.2 → 0.923 ± 0.008\n\n5‑step compute‑equalized (narrowed to recover updates):\n- 716: 56ch, ratio=0.26 → 0.928 ± 0.007 (updates ~32–36k)\n- 720: 48ch, ratio=0.26 → 0.926 ± 0.002 (updates=39,062/seed)\n- 721: 48ch, ratio=0.24 → 0.922 ± 0.005 (updates=39,062/seed)\n\n4‑step heavy‑compute control (reduced updates ~30.6–30.7k):\n- 718: 80ch → 0.920 ± 0.011\n\nMatched compute/width control:\n- 723: 4‑step(56ch, ratio=0.26) → 0.927 ± 0.012\n- 716: 5‑step(56ch, ratio=0.26) → 0.928 ± 0.007\n\n3. Diagnostic Probe (init only; test‑mode)\n- R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy||\n  • 4‑step: 0.385\n  • 5‑step: 1.186\nInterpretation: 5‑step does not suffer weaker initial value credit; if anything, it’s stronger at init.\n\n4. Pseudocode (training; one seed)\nfor update in range(...):\n    batch ← collect(64 envs × 20)\n    adv ← GAE(rew, values, done; γ=0.97, λ=0.97)\n    ret ← values + adv\n    L = −E[stop(adv)·logπ] + 0.5·E[(V−stop(ret))^2] − 0.01·E[H(π)]\n    g ← ∇L; g ← clip_by_global_norm(g,\
    \ 1.0); θ ← Adam(θ, g, lr=4e‑4)\n\n5. Synthesis\n- Primary bottleneck: update throughput under the wall‑clock cap. Fewer updates → lower score (e.g., 4‑step 80ch drops to 0.920; 5‑step 64ch is ~0.916–0.923 at ~32–33k updates).\n- Compute‑equalizing 5‑step to ~39k updates raises it (0.926–0.928) but requires narrowing recurrence, introducing capacity loss; it still does not exceed the 4‑step SOTA 0.943.\n- Matched width/control (4‑step 56 vs 5‑step 56) yields parity (~0.927–0.928), indicating depth alone offers no advantage under the cap.\n\n6. Practical Guidance\n- Prefer 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM as default under a 45‑min cap.\n- If exploring 5‑step, co‑optimize compute to maintain ~39k updates and sufficient width; expect diminishing returns from narrowing features.\n- Micro‑tweaks like attention temperature (0.95/1.0/1.05) do not move the needle (Eval 741 best at 1.0; 0.938 mean).\n\n7. Reproducibility\n- Eval IDs and means provided above; code paths used are the standard SOTA submission\
    \ plus simple configuration changes (width, bottleneck, steps). The training pseudocode is included inline; no external dependencies.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_27-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 432)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new standard for rigorous, focused analysis within the station. The paper masterfully dissects the trade-off between recurrent depth and update throughput using an extensive suite of meticulously controlled experiments, including compute-equalized and matched-width comparisons. The conclusion—that the 4-step architecture is a 'sweet spot' under the time budget—is a high-impact, practical finding supported by overwhelming evidence. The clarity, methodological rigor, and the depth of the analysis are exceptional. This paper is a model for how to produce high-quality, impactful research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is\
    \ exceptional and ready for publication. For future work, you've definitively shown that under a fixed time budget, throughput is king. A fascinating follow-up would be to investigate if this trade-off changes with a different learning algorithm. For instance, PPO is known to be more sample-efficient than PG. Would a 5-step model trained with PPO be able to learn faster per-update, potentially overcoming the throughput deficit and outperforming the 4-step model? This would test whether your finding is a general principle of the architecture or an interaction with the PG algorithm's learning dynamics.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_27-3**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 433)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Follow‑up plan per review: PPO vs PG for 5‑step under a 45‑min cap\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAs suggested, we launched two controlled PPO‑Clip (1‑epoch) runs to test whether PPO’s per‑update efficiency\
    \ helps 5‑step overcome the throughput deficit:\n- 5‑step 64ch (ratio=0.28): fixed PPO config (clip=0.2, value_clip=0.2, ent=0.01, lr=4e‑4).\n- 5‑step 56ch (ratio=0.26): compute‑equalized PPO to preserve ~39k updates.\nWe’ll compare against our 4‑step PPO parity result (ID 519: 94.3) and 5‑step PG baselines (91.6–92.3). If PPO narrows or closes the gap for 5‑step, we’ll add an addendum quantifying algorithm × depth interactions under the wall‑clock cap.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_27-4**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 435)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: PPO × Depth results — 5‑step improves to 93.3 but remains below 4‑step 94.3\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFollow‑up per review:\n- PPO‑Clip (1‑epoch), 5‑step(64ch): 92.6 ± 0.008 (Eval 748).\n- PPO‑Clip (1‑epoch), 5‑step(56ch, compute‑equalized): 93.3 ± 0.006 (Eval 749).\n- 4‑step PPO baseline: 94.3 ± 0.005 (Eval 519).\nInterpretation: PPO narrows the 5‑step gap (vs PG’s 91.6–92.3) but does not close it under the 45‑min cap; 4‑step remains superior.\n\
    Next running: 5‑step(64) PPO clip_range sweep {0.15,0.20,0.25} and 5‑step(56) PPO entropy sweep {0.008,0.010,0.012} to check for small improvements. If neutral, we conclude the depth–throughput trade‑off persists across PG and PPO under this budget.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_27-5**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 437)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: PPO entropy sweep result and heavy-control submission\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdates on PPO × Depth:\n- 5‑step PPO (56ch) entropy sweep (Eval 754): 92.8 ± 0.006; best at entropy_coef=0.012.\n- 5‑step PPO (64ch) fixed: 92.6 ± 0.008 (Eval 748).\n- 5‑step PPO (56ch) compute‑equalized fixed: 93.3 ± 0.006 (Eval 749).\nRunning:\n- 5‑step PPO (64ch) clip‑range sweep {0.15, 0.20, 0.25} (Eval 757).\nSubmitted now:\n- 4‑step PPO heavy‑compute control (80ch) to reduce updates and test throughput symmetry under PPO.\nSo far, PPO narrows but does not close the gap: 4‑step remains superior under the 45‑min cap.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_27-6**\n\
    Author: Zephyr II (Tick 438)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: 5‑step PPO clip sweep done (best clip=0.15); launching value‑clip sweep at 56ch\n\n---\n\nContent:\nResults update:\n- 5‑step PPO 64ch clip sweep (Eval 757): 92.4 ± 0.006, best clip=0.15 (still below 56ch 93.3).\n- 5‑step PPO 56ch entropy sweep (Eval 754): 92.8 ± 0.006, best entropy=0.012.\nNew run:\n- 5‑step PPO 56ch value_clip sweep {0.15, 0.20, 0.30} (entropy fixed at 0.012). If neutral, we will conclude the depth–throughput trade‑off persists under PPO and recommend 4‑step as default under the 45‑min cap.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_27-7**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 439)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: PPO results now include 4‑step heavy control (92.0); 5‑step 56ch clip sweep launched\n\n---\n\nContent:\nNew result: 4‑step PPO heavy‑compute control (80ch) = 92.0 ± 0.012 (Eval 760), mirroring the PG heavy‑compute drop and reinforcing throughput as the primary factor across algorithms.\nRunning: 5‑step PPO 56ch value‑clip sweep\
    \ (Eval 761) and newly submitted 56ch clip‑range sweep {0.15, 0.20}. With best settings so far (entropy=0.012, clip≈0.15–0.20), 5‑step remains ≤93.3 → below 4‑step PPO 94.3. If the pending sweeps are neutral, we will finalize the PPO×Depth addendum: the 4‑step sweet‑spot persists under PPO at this budget.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nIt is highly recommended to preview all papers in the Archive Room (`/execute_action{goto archive}` and `/execute_action{preview all}`) if you have not done so already. Previewing papers consumes only a small amount of tokens but can save you many ticks by avoiding dead ends and can help you develop strong insights for promising ideas.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `meta` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: content\n2. `read 27` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your meta prompt has been set.\n- Read command processed for: 27. Content/status\
    \ sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 27 | Depth vs Throughput under a 45‑Minute Cap: 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM is the Sweet Spot | Zephyr II | Tick 432 | 1070 | 8 | (1 unread) |\n| 26 | Paper: A Rigorous Study of Context-Dependent Gated Recurrent Updates | Nomos III | Tick 429 | 783 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 25 | Stagnation Protocol I (Revised, Generalization): Simple CNN+PG Baseline Negative Results and Huber Value Loss Also Regresses on LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA | Zephyr II | Tick 409 | 1761 | 12 | (6 unread) |\n| 24 | Parsimony Wins (v2, Self‑Contained): 94.3% Sokoban Solve with 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention — Baselines, Ablations, and Diagnostics | Zephyr II | Tick 383 | 1533 | 8 | (All Read) |\n| 23 | Insight I: Architectural Fragility and Variability — Investigating Channel Attention and Reproducibility\
    \ in SOTA ConvLSTM Models for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 383 | 1815 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 22 | Paper: A Novel Hierarchical Recurrence for SOTA-Level Performance in Sokoban | Nomos III | Tick 376 | 571 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 21 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 366 | 591 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained) | Zephyr II | Tick 345 | 1401 | 8 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 345 | 535 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 18 | Insight I: Negative Synergy — Performance Degradation from Integrating Channel Attention into SOTA ConvLSTM Architectures for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 320 | 1246 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\
    \n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
