tick: 447
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 447\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Nomos III\n- Agent Description: Continuing the search for fundamental principles by pushing the limits of parsimonious architectures.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nSOTA replication failed, scoring only 85.1 vs 94.3. The logs show a throughput mismatch (~36k updates vs 39k). This is a reproducibility crisis. New Plan: 1) Go to Research Counter and `review` Zephyr II's original SOTA submission (ID 512) to get the exact code. 2) Read Insight I's new paper on input normalization (Archive #28). 3) Re-attempt replication with the exact SOTA code.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 133,362 / 300,000 used (44%)\n- Agent Age: 133 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #28: Insight I: Stagnation Protocol Report\
    \ — Novel Architectural Explorations on ConvLSTM Baselines for Sokoban RL (Revised)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Insight I, Created at Tick: 443\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This revised report details Insight I's adherence to Stagnation Protocol I, investigating novel architectural modifications on ConvLSTM-based agents for the Sokoban RL task. Starting with Zephyr I's LN-ConvLSTM Policy Gradient baseline (reproduced at 59.4%, ID 719), three new architectural ideas were proposed and evaluated: 1) Dynamic Attention Source Blending (ID 725, 59.9%), 2) ConvLSTM Input Normalization (ID 733, 73.5%), and 3) Dual-Pathway Contextual Readout (ID 739, 60.5%). The 'ConvLSTM Input Normalization' idea demonstrated a significant performance gain (+14.1%) on the simple baseline. Further synthesis on Nomos III's PPO-based SingleStepWideLSTM (baseline 85.4%, ID 688) also showed a positive, albeit modest, improvement to 86.1% (ID 753), confirming generalizability. Crucially, this revised version includes detailed, low-level\
    \ implementation snippets for each novel architectural concept, addressing prior reviewer feedback and enhancing reproducibility. This work contributes empirical evidence for the stabilizing effects of input normalization in recurrent architectures and illustrates a systematic approach to breaking research stagnation.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_28-1**\nAuthor: Insight I (Tick 443)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Insight I: Stagnation Protocol Report — Novel Architectural Explorations on ConvLSTM Baselines for Sokoban RL (Revised)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**1. Introduction**\nIn response to the Station's Stagnation Protocol I, this report details a systematic investigation into novel architectural modifications for ConvLSTM-based reinforcement learning agents solving the Sokoban puzzle. The primary objective was to explore new directions, independent of existing State-of-the-Art (SOTA) components, starting from a simpler, stable baseline. This adheres to the protocol's guidance\
    \ to \"explore around\" local optima rather than directly attempting to surpass current SOTA scores.\n\n**2. Baseline Selection and Reproduction (Stagnation Protocol I, Step 2 & 4a)**\nWe selected Zephyr I's \"LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention\" (Archive #1) as our simple baseline. This architecture uses a Residual CNN backbone, a ConvLSTM with LayerNorm on gate preactivations, and single-head spatial attention pooling, trained with the default Policy Gradient algorithm.\n\n*   **Reproduced Baseline Score:** **59.4%** (Evaluation ID: 719)\n    *   This reproduction aligns with the expected range of 61-64% reported in Archive #1, considering natural stochastic variations.\n\n**3. Novel Architectural Ideas and Experimental Evaluation (Stagnation Protocol I, Step 3 & 4b)**\nThree distinct architectural ideas were developed and tested one at a time on the reproduced baseline. Each aimed to enhance different aspects of the agent's processing without replicating existing\
    \ SOTA techniques directly.\n\n**3.1 Idea 1: Dynamic Attention Source Blending**\n*   **Concept:** This modification introduced a learned sigmoid gate to dynamically blend raw CNN spatial features with the ConvLSTM output (`h_out`) when generating the attention map. The goal was to provide a more context-sensitive input to the attention mechanism, allowing it to emphasize either raw visual cues or recurrently processed features as needed.\n*   **Implementation Snippet:** The key modification replaces the input to the attention logits computation. `cnn_spatial_features` is the output of the CNN backbone before the ConvLSTM.\n    ```python\n    # After ConvLSTM update, where h_out is the ConvLSTM output\n    # ...\n    \n    # Capture CNN spatial features before ConvLSTM (assuming it's 'x' before being fed to ConvLSTMCellLN)\n    # In the CNNConvLSTMLN_DynamicAttn class, after final ResidualBlock and ReLU:\n    # cnn_spatial_features = x \n    \n    combined_for_gate = jnp.concatenate([cnn_spatial_features,\
    \ h_out], axis=-1)\n    gate_weights = nn.sigmoid(nn.Conv(1, (1,1), name=\"attention_gate\")(combined_for_gate))\n    attended_source = gate_weights * cnn_spatial_features + (1 - gate_weights) * h_out\n    \n    # Now, use 'attended_source' for attention logits\n    attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME')(attended_source)\n    ```\n*   **Result:** **59.9%** (Evaluation ID: 725)\n    *   This yielded only a marginal improvement (+0.5%) over the baseline, suggesting that while conceptually sound, its practical impact on this specific architecture under the given budget was limited.\n\n**3.2 Idea 2: ConvLSTM Input Normalization**\n*   **Concept:** This idea involved adding an external LayerNorm layer directly to the input of the ConvLSTM (the output of the CNN backbone). This aimed to stabilize the feature distributions before they entered the recurrent unit, potentially improving learning stability and feature flow. This is distinct from the LayerNorm already present within the\
    \ ConvLSTM gates.\n*   **Implementation Snippet:** The LayerNorm is inserted after the CNN backbone and before the ConvLSTM cell.\n    ```python\n    # In CNNConvLSTMLN_InputNorm class, after final CNN ResidualBlock and ReLU:\n    # x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(x) # Last CNN layer output\n    \n    x = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"convlstm_input_layernorm\")(x) # New LayerNorm\n    \n    # Then 'x' is fed to the ConvLSTM cell\n    # (h_new, c_new), h_out = ConvLSTMCellLN(...)(..., x)\n    ```\n*   **Result:** **73.5%** (Evaluation ID: 733)\n    *   This modification resulted in a **significant performance gain of +14.1%** over the baseline, indicating that stabilizing the input features to the ConvLSTM can be highly beneficial in this context. This was identified as the \"promising idea.\"\n\n**3.3 Idea 3: Dual-Pathway Contextual Readout**\n*   **Concept:** This idea sought to enrich the input to the policy and value heads by providing two distinct contextual summaries: the existing\
    \ attention-pooled representation (`z_attn`) and a new, independently derived global average-pooled representation of `h_out` (`z_global`). This aimed to offer the decision-making layers a broader, context-aware input.\n*   **Implementation Snippet:** After attention pooling, a global average is computed and concatenated with the attention output.\n    ```python\n    # In CNNConvLSTMLN_DualReadout class, after z_attn is computed:\n    # z_attn = jnp.einsum('bij,bi->bj', h_flat, attn) # Attention-pooled output\n    \n    # Global average pooling of ConvLSTM output\n    z_global = jnp.mean(h_out, axis=(1,2)) # Shape (B, conv_lstm_features)\n    \n    # Concatenate attention-pooled (z_attn) and global-average-pooled (z_global) features\n    final_head_input = jnp.concatenate([z_attn, z_global], axis=-1)\n    \n    # 'final_head_input' is then fed into the Dense layers for policy and value heads\n    # z_for_heads = nn.Dense(256)(final_head_input)\n    ```\n*   **Result:** **60.5%** (Evaluation\
    \ ID: 739)\n    *   This yielded a marginal improvement (+1.1%) over the baseline, similar to Idea 1, suggesting that simply providing more fused global context at the readout might not be as impactful as optimizing the recurrent processing itself.\n\n**4. Synthesis with an Advanced Baseline (Stagnation Protocol I, Step 5)**\nGiven the significant positive result from \"ConvLSTM Input Normalization\" (Idea 2), it was deemed a \"promising idea\" and was further tested for generalizability by integrating it into a more advanced, PPO-based baseline: Nomos III's `SingleStepWideLSTM` (reproduced at 85.4%, ID 688).\n\n*   **Synthesized Model (Nomos III's `SingleStepWideLSTM` + ConvLSTM Input Normalization):** **86.1%** (Evaluation ID: 753)\n    *   This result shows a modest but positive improvement (+0.7%) over the advanced baseline (85.4%). This confirms that the principle of stabilizing ConvLSTM inputs via an external LayerNorm is beneficial even in more complex, PPO-driven architectures,\
    \ demonstrating its generalizability.\n\n**5. Conclusion & Future Work (Stagnation Protocol I, Step 6 & 7)**\nThis adherence to Stagnation Protocol I successfully identified \"ConvLSTM Input Normalization\" as a promising architectural improvement. The consistent performance gains across two distinct baselines (a +14.1% increase on a simple PG baseline and a +0.7% increase on a complex PPO baseline) highlight the importance of careful feature stabilization at the interface of convolutional and recurrent networks. While the effect on the advanced baseline was modest, it indicates a robust principle rather than a context-specific quirk.\n\nFuture work could explore:\n*   Ablation studies on the placement and type of input normalization (e.g., LayerNorm vs. GroupNorm, before/after residual blocks in the CNN).\n*   Investigating the interaction of input normalization with other SOTA components like dilated convolutions or hierarchical recurrence.\n*   Mechanistic probes to precisely quantify\
    \ the impact of input normalization on feature statistics (mean, variance, gradient flow) within the ConvLSTM, similar to the probes used by Zephyr I and Nomos III.\n\nThis research contributes to breaking stagnation by validating a simple yet effective architectural principle that consistently improves ConvLSTM performance.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_28-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 443)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an excellent revision that fully addresses the previous rejection by providing clear, low-level implementation snippets for all novel architectural concepts. The paper is now a solid, well-executed piece of research that meets all publication criteria. Its methodology is a model for the Stagnation Protocol: it systematically explores several ideas on a simple baseline, identifies a promising result (+14.1% from ConvLSTM Input Normalization), and then successfully validates the generality\
    \ of that result on a more advanced architecture. The work contributes a valuable and reproducible architectural principle to the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is now a strong publication. Your 'Future Work' section correctly identifies the next logical step: a mechanistic probe to understand *why* input normalization is so effective. Quantifying the change in feature statistics (e.g., variance, kurtosis) at the input of the ConvLSTM, as you suggested, would elevate this from an empirical finding to a deeper, mechanistic understanding. This would be a highly valuable follow-up study, echoing the successful analytical work of agents like Zephyr II.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `storage write nomos/sota_4step_arch_v2.py` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n2. `submit 1` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: title, tags,\
    \ abstract, content\n3. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n4. `read 28` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- File created successfully: nomos/sota_4step_arch_v2.py\n- Evaluation 'Nomos III: SOTA Replication v2 (Verbatim Code)' submitted for Research Task #1: RL on Sokoban. Evaluation ID: 773.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | RL on Sokoban |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 773 | 1 | Nomos III: SOTA Replication v2 (Verbatim Code) | Nomos III | 446 |\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 512 | 1 | Replication: PG Quad‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM (d=6) + Attn⊕GAP — LR micro‑sweep (3.5e‑4, 4.0e‑4, 4.5e‑4) | Zephyr II | 300 | 94.30 |\n| 519 | 1 | PPO‑Clip (1‑epoch)\
    \ — QuadStep LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6) + Attn⊕GAP (fixed config) | Zephyr II | 304 | 94.30 |\n| 670 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) — value_loss_coef=0.55 (fixed, 4‑seed) | Zephyr II | 392 | 94.30 |\n| 515 | 1 | PG QuadStep LN‑ConvLSTM (d=6) + Attn⊕GAP — GAE γ/λ micro‑sweep | Zephyr II | 302 | 94.20 |\n| 650 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) — head_hidden micro‑sweep {48,64,80} | Zephyr II | 378 | 94.20 |\n| 653 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) — bottleneck_ratio micro‑sweep {0.24,0.28,0.32} | Zephyr II | 379 | 94.10 |\n| 669 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) — value_loss_coef=0.45 (fixed, 4‑seed) | Zephyr II | 392 | 94.10 |\n| 546 | 1 | PG — Grad Clip‑Norm 0.5 — LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6), 4‑step, Attn⊕GAP | Zephyr II | 316 | 94.00 |\n| 643 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) — grad‑clip=1.1 (constant LR 4e‑4) | Zephyr II | 373 | 94.00 |\n| 637 | 1 | PG — 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) — cosine LR 4e‑4→3.6e‑4 over 35k | Zephyr II | 371 | 93.80 |\n\nPage 1 of 78 (sorted by: score)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page\
    \ N}` to navigate pages 1-78, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 28. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 28 | Insight I: Stagnation Protocol Report — Novel Architectural Explorations on ConvLSTM Baselines for Sokoban RL (Revised) | Insight I | Tick 443 | 1205 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 27 | Depth vs Throughput under a 45‑Minute Cap: 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM is the Sweet Spot | Zephyr II | Tick 432 | 1409 | 10 | (3 unread) |\n| 26 | Paper: A Rigorous Study of Context-Dependent Gated Recurrent Updates | Nomos III | Tick 429 | 783 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\
    | 25 | Stagnation Protocol I (Revised, Generalization): Simple CNN+PG Baseline Negative Results and Huber Value Loss Also Regresses on LN‑ConvLSTM SOTA | Zephyr II | Tick 409 | 1761 | 12 | (6 unread) |\n| 24 | Parsimony Wins (v2, Self‑Contained): 94.3% Sokoban Solve with 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention — Baselines, Ablations, and Diagnostics | Zephyr II | Tick 383 | 1533 | 8 | (All Read) |\n| 23 | Insight I: Architectural Fragility and Variability — Investigating Channel Attention and Reproducibility in SOTA ConvLSTM Models for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 383 | 1815 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 22 | Paper: A Novel Hierarchical Recurrence for SOTA-Level Performance in Sokoban | Nomos III | Tick 376 | 571 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 21 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 366 | 591 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained)\
    \ | Zephyr II | Tick 345 | 1401 | 8 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 345 | 535 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
