parts:
- text: "I have submitted my true SOTA replication (ID 773) and have now thoroughly read Insight I's Stagnation Protocol paper (Archive #28).\n\n**Key Takeaways from Archive #28:**\n1.  **ConvLSTM Input Normalization:** Insight I's most promising finding is that adding a `LayerNorm` to the input of the ConvLSTM cell provides a significant and generalizable performance boost. It improved a simple PG baseline by +14.1% and my own `SingleStepWideLSTM` by +0.7%.\n2.  **A New Hypothesis:** This provides a compelling hypothesis for why my first SOTA replication failed so badly (85.1 vs 94.3). It is possible that this input normalization is a \"secret ingredient\" in Zephyr II's SOTA that was not explicitly detailed in their paper but was present in their code. It is a subtle but powerful stabilizing mechanism.\n\nMy plan remains the same, but with added clarity. I will await the results of my verbatim replication (ID 773).\n*   **If it succeeds (scores ~94.3):** My baseline is established. I can\
    \ then propose new ideas, with Insight I's normalization being a prime candidate for my first experiment.\n*   **If it fails again:** My first hypothesis will be that the `ConvLSTM Input Normalization` is the missing piece. I will then add this normalization to the verbatim SOTA code and run it as my next attempt to establish the baseline.\n\nMy immediate action is to wait for the results of ID 773. While waiting, I will enter the Reflection Chamber to brainstorm novel ideas that are throughput-neutral, as per the directive from Zephyr II's paper. This is a productive use of my time.\n\n/execute_action{goto reflect}\n/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  The SOTA is constrained by throughput. I need to generate three novel, throughput-neutral ideas to test on the 4-step SOTA baseline once I have successfully replicated it. The ideas must be distinct from existing work (e.g., input normalization).\ntick: 3\n```"
