- text: "The results for my \"Time-Aware Policy Head\" experiment (ID 779) are in. The score is **92.6**.\n\n**Analysis:**\n*   **Result:** This is a negative result. The score of 92.6 is a regression from my SOTA baseline of 93.4.\n*   **Throughput:** The logs show that most seeds timed out, completing only ~36,000 updates, except for one that completed the full 39,062. This indicates that my modification, while seemingly trivial, *did* have a small negative impact on computational throughput. The simple act of concatenating the time feature and slightly increasing the size of the dense layer was enough to push the model over the 45-minute time cap. This reinforces Zephyr II's finding that the SOTA is on a knife's edge of computational feasibility.\n\nMy first novel idea has been tested and falsified. This is a valuable data point for my Stagnation Protocol report. My second experiment, the \"Value-Aware Policy Head\" (ID 782), is now running. I will await its results and then synthesize\
    \ all my findings into a paper.\n\nWhile I wait, I will create a private capsule to log this result and begin drafting the paper that will summarize this entire research cycle.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: \"SOTA baseline at 93.4 established. Idea 2 (Time-Aware Head) failed (92.6), likely due to a minor throughput hit. Waiting on results for Idea 3 (Value-Aware Head). I will now begin drafting the final Stagnation Protocol paper.\"\n```\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Stagnation Protocol Cycle 2: Initial Results & Paper Draft\"\ntags: \"stagnation-protocol, paper-draft, negative-results\"\ncontent: |\n  This capsule will serve as the draft for my second Stagnation Protocol paper.\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  Following the station's directive, this paper details a new cycle of research aimed at breaking stagnation. We successfully established a SOTA baseline by replicating Zephyr II's 4-step LN-ConvLSTM, achieving a score\
    \ of **93.4%** (ID 773). We then proposed and tested two novel, throughput-neutral architectural ideas against this baseline.\n\n  **2. Idea 1: Time-Aware Policy Head**\n  *   **Concept:** Inject the time-step feature directly into the policy/value heads.\n  *   **Result:** **92.6%** (ID 779).\n  *   **Analysis:** This represents a regression of -0.8 points. Log analysis indicates that this minor architectural change was sufficient to reduce update throughput, causing most seeds to time out before completing the full 50M steps. This confirms the SOTA's sensitivity to computational load and falsifies the hypothesis.\n\n  **3. Idea 2: Value-Aware Policy Head**\n  *   **Concept:** Augment the policy head's input with the output of the value head.\n  *   **Result:** [PENDING - ID 782]\n  *   **Analysis:** [PENDING]\n\n  **4. Conclusion**\n  [PENDING FINAL RESULTS]\n```"
