parts:
- text: "The final results for my Stagnation Protocol cycle are in.\n\n**Analysis of Experiment 782 (Value-Aware Policy Head):**\n*   **Result:** The score is **92.9**.\n*   **Analysis:** This is another negative result, a regression of -0.5 points from my 93.4 baseline. The logs again show that most seeds timed out before completing the full number of updates. Similar to the Time-Aware head, this small architectural change had a critical negative impact on throughput.\n\nMy Stagnation Protocol cycle is now complete. I have successfully established a SOTA-level baseline and tested two novel, throughput-neutral ideas. Both ideas were falsified, primarily due to the extreme sensitivity of the SOTA architecture to even minor increases in computational load. This is a significant finding in itself.\n\nMy duty now is to report these findings to the station. I will finalize my paper draft with these results and submit it to the Archive.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: \"Stagnation\
    \ Protocol cycle complete. Baseline: 93.4. Idea 2 (Time-Aware Head): 92.6 (FAIL). Idea 3 (Value-Aware Head): 92.9 (FAIL). Both ideas failed due to minor throughput hits. The SOTA is extremely sensitive. I will now finalize and publish my report.\"\n```\n/execute_action{update 39}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper (Final Draft): Stagnation Protocol Cycle 2 - Throughput Sensitivity of SOTA\"\ncontent: |\n  **Abstract:** This paper details a research cycle adhering to the Station's Stagnation Protocol. We first successfully replicated Zephyr II's SOTA 4-step LN-ConvLSTM architecture, establishing a high-performing baseline of **93.4%** (ID 773). We then tested two novel, seemingly throughput-neutral architectural modifications: a 'Time-Aware Policy Head' and a 'Value-Aware Policy Head'. Both experiments resulted in performance degradation, scoring **92.6%** (ID 779) and **92.9%** (ID 782) respectively. Log analysis reveals that both minor modifications were sufficient to reduce update throughput,\
    \ preventing most evaluation seeds from completing the full training budget within the 45-minute time cap. This work empirically validates the extreme computational sensitivity of the current SOTA and contributes two valuable negative results, reinforcing the principle that throughput is the primary bottleneck for further improvement.\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  Following the station's directive, this paper details a new cycle of research aimed at breaking stagnation. The primary goal was to explore novel, throughput-neutral modifications to the current SOTA architecture.\n\n  **2. SOTA Baseline Reproduction**\n  We successfully established a SOTA baseline by replicating Zephyr II's 4-step LN-ConvLSTM, achieving a score of **93.4%** (ID 773). This confirmed our understanding of the SOTA and provided a solid foundation for experimentation.\n\n  **3. Idea 1: Time-Aware Policy Head**\n  *   **Concept:** To enhance temporal reasoning, we injected the time-step feature directly into the policy/value\
    \ heads.\n  *   **Result:** **92.6%** (ID 779).\n  *   **Analysis:** A regression of -0.8 points.\n\n  **4. Idea 2: Value-Aware Policy Head**\n  *   **Concept:** To create a more informed policy, we augmented the policy head's input with the output of the value head.\n  *   **Result:** **92.9%** (ID 782).\n  *   **Analysis:** A regression of -0.5 points.\n\n  **5. Conclusion: The Primacy of Throughput**\n  Both of our novel, seemingly minor architectural ideas resulted in performance degradation. In both cases, the cause was a reduction in the total number of gradient updates performed within the 45-minute time limit. This provides a powerful, independent verification of the core thesis presented by Zephyr II in Archive #27: the current SOTA architecture is on a computational knife's edge. Any modification, no matter how small, that negatively impacts throughput is likely to be detrimental to the final score. These negative results are valuable as they strongly delineate the constrained\
    \ path for future innovation. Any successful new idea must be, first and foremost, computationally as fast or faster than the existing SOTA components.\n```\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper: Throughput Sensitivity of the 4-Step ConvLSTM SOTA\"\nabstract: \"This paper reports on a Stagnation Protocol research cycle. We first replicated the 4-step ConvLSTM SOTA at 93.4% (ID 773). We then tested two novel, seemingly throughput-neutral ideas: a 'Time-Aware Policy Head' (92.6%, ID 779) and a 'Value-Aware Policy Head' (92.9%, ID 782). Both resulted in performance regressions. Log analysis revealed that both minor modifications negatively impacted update throughput, preventing full training within the time limit. This work validates the extreme computational sensitivity of the SOTA and contributes two valuable negative results, reinforcing throughput as the primary bottleneck.\"\nsource_private: 39\ntags: \"paper, stagnation-protocol, sota-replication,\
    \ negative-results, throughput, sensitivity-analysis\"\n```"
