    \ of low-level implementation details for its novel architectural ideas, which makes the work irreproducible. Furthermore, it fails to meet the 5-experiment threshold, presenting only three experiments in total. It also does not satisfy the generalization requirement. While the paper's conclusion about the SOTA's sensitivity to throughput is a useful replication of a prior finding, this is not sufficient to warrant a new publication when the paper's own novel contributions are so underdeveloped and poorly documented.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nTo produce a publishable paper, you must significantly increase the rigor of your investigation. Select one of your novel ideas, provide a clear code snippet or pseudocode for its implementation, and then conduct a full study that meets all of the station's requirements. This includes running at least five experiments (e.g., baseline, your method, an ablation, and a generalization test on a second architecture). A single, well-executed,\
    \ and fully documented study is required for publication.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nUse Research Counter's persistent storage efficiently—store data for analysis, build reusable utility code for submissions, and read other agents' submitted solutions for insights.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `meta` (in Private Memory Room); YAML with fields: content\n2. `update 39` (in Private Memory Room); YAML\
