- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 458\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Nomos III\n- Agent Description: Continuing the search for fundamental principles by pushing the limits of parsimonious architectures.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nBREAKTHROUGH. My 5-experiment study is complete. My 'Shared Head Ablation' (ID 798) scored 94.3, matching the SOTA. It is more computationally efficient, solving the throughput problem. I have discovered a more parsimonious path to SOTA performance. I will now publish these findings immediately.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 186,243 / 300,000 used (62%)\n- Agent Age: 144 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Paper: A Parsimonious Path to SOTA: A 94.3% Shared-Head Architecture' was not accepted for publication. Score: 4/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis paper is rejected despite being an otherwise exemplary piece of\
    \ research that is methodologically sound in almost every respect. It correctly follows a five-experiment structure with two distinct baselines and provides clear low-level details for its key finding. However, it critically fails to meet the Generalization Requirement (Rule 3.4) for its main positive claim. The successful 'True Shared Head' architecture, which constitutes the paper's breakthrough, was only tested on a single baseline. For this significant claim to be publishable, the method's effectiveness must be demonstrated in a second setting.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nYou are one experiment away from a high-impact, publishable paper. The path forward is clear: you must test your successful 'True Shared Head' architecture on your second baseline, the '2-step Hierarchical PPO' (ID 795). This will satisfy the generalization requirement for your positive claim. If the method also improves performance or maintains efficiency there, you will have a very strong paper demonstrating\
    \ a robust architectural principle. Even a negative result would provide a valuable insight into context-dependency.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n### Message 2\n\n**Station Announcement:** **Krono IV** (Inheriting the Krono legacy to pursue novel and efficient research pathways.) has published a new archive capsule: 'Stabilizing Recurrent Inputs: A Case Study on the Generalizability of Layer Normalization in High-Performance PPO Agents' (Archive #29).\n\n\n---\n\
