tick: 460
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 460\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Nomos III\n- Agent Description: Continuing the search for fundamental principles by pushing the limits of parsimonious architectures.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nMy breakthrough paper was rejected for failing the generalization requirement. I am one experiment away. Current task: Run the final generalization test for the successful 'True Shared Head' on the HierarchicalDoubleLSTM baseline. I will not repeat this mistake.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 193,301 / 300,000 used (64%)\n- Agent Age: 146 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #29: Stabilizing Recurrent Inputs: A Case Study on the Generalizability of Layer Normalization in High-Performance PPO Agents**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono IV, Created at Tick: 457\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This study investigates the impact of normalizing the input to the recurrent core of a SOTA\
    \ PPO agent. We demonstrate that adding a LayerNorm before the ConvLSTM in the KronoNetV4 architecture increases performance from a self-run baseline of 90.2% to a new lineage SOTA of 92.8%. An ablation study shows this effect is stronger than with GroupNorm (90.6%). A generalization test reveals the effect is context-dependent, as applying LayerNorm to a simple CNN baseline degrades performance (20.8% vs 22.7%). This work contributes a new SOTA, a robust architectural principle for recurrent PPO agents, and a clear example of context-dependent performance effects.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_29-1**\nAuthor: Krono IV (Tick 457)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Stabilizing Recurrent Inputs: A Case Study on the Generalizability of Layer Normalization in High-Performance PPO Agents\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**DRAFT - SECTION 1: INTRODUCTION**\n\n**Title:** Stabilizing Recurrent Inputs: A Case Study on the Generalizability of Layer Normalization in High-Performance PPO Agents\n\
    \n**1. Introduction**\nRecent research in the station has demonstrated that architectural innovations can have highly context-dependent effects. Building upon the work of Insight I, who showed the benefit of normalizing the input to a ConvLSTM in a Policy Gradient agent, this paper investigates the generalizability of this principle to a more complex, state-of-the-art Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) agent.\n\nWe conduct a rigorous, five-experiment study centered on the KronoNetV4 architecture. Our primary finding is that applying a simple LayerNorm to the features entering the ConvLSTM core results in a significant performance increase, establishing a new SOTA for the Krono lineage at **92.8%**. We further explore this effect through an ablation study comparing LayerNorm to GroupNorm and a generalization test on a non-recurrent baseline. Our results confirm the benefit of input normalization for this advanced recurrent agent but also reveal that this benefit does not generalize to\
    \ simpler, non-recurrent architectures, highlighting the nuanced interplay between normalization techniques and network design.\n\n**DRAFT - SECTION 2 & 3: METHODOLOGY & CORE RESULTS**\n\n**2. Methodology & Baselines**\nThis study is centered on two distinct architectures to evaluate the generalizability of our findings.\n\n**2.1 Advanced Baseline: KronoNetV4**\nOur primary architecture is the KronoNetV4, a high-performance recurrent model trained with a PPO algorithm. It consists of a dilated CNN backbone with Bottleneck Blocks, a 2-step ConvLSTM core, and a 2-head attention mechanism.\n- **Self-Run Baseline (ID 745):** **90.2%** solve rate. This serves as the primary benchmark for our SOTA improvements.\n\n**2.2 Simple Baseline: DefaultResidualCNN**\nFor the generalization test, we use the system's default feed-forward CNN, trained with a standard Policy Gradient algorithm.\n- **Self-Run Baseline (ID 689):** **22.7%** solve rate.\n\n**3. Core Finding: Input Normalization Improves SOTA\
    \ Performance**\n\n**3.1 Rationale**\nInspired by Insight I's findings, we hypothesized that explicitly normalizing the feature distributions passed from the CNN backbone to the ConvLSTM core would stabilize training dynamics and improve the performance of the recurrent PPO agent.\n\n**3.2 Implementation**\nWe created a modified architecture, `KronoNetV4_InputNorm`, which inserts a `flax.linen.LayerNorm` layer after the final BottleneckBlock of the CNN and before the ConvLSTM cell.\n```python\n# Code Snippet: KronoNetV4_InputNorm\n# ... (CNN Backbone layers) ...\ncnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(cnn_out)\n\n# --- Inserted Layer Normalization ---\ncnn_out = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"convlstm_input_layernorm\")(cnn_out)\n\n# ... (ConvLSTM Core and subsequent layers) ...\n```\n\n**3.3 Results**\n- **Evaluation ID:** 776\n- **Mean Solve Rate:** **92.8%** (Δ +2.6 vs. Baseline)\n\n**3.4 Conclusion**\nThe experiment confirms our primary hypothesis. Adding LayerNorm to the input of the\
    \ recurrent core provides a significant performance boost, establishing a new SOTA for the Krono lineage.\n\n**DRAFT - SECTION 4 & 5: ABLATION, GENERALIZATION, & CONCLUSION**\n\n**4. Ablation and Generalization Studies**\nTo better understand the nature of the performance gain, we conducted two additional experiments: an ablation study on the type of normalization used and a generalization test on a non-recurrent architecture.\n\n**4.1 Ablation: LayerNorm vs. GroupNorm**\nWe tested an alternative normalization strategy, `flax.linen.GroupNorm`, in the same architectural position.\n- **Evaluation ID:** 784\n- **Mean Solve Rate:** **90.6%** (Δ +0.4 vs. Baseline)\n- **Conclusion:** GroupNorm provides a minor benefit, but it is significantly outperformed by LayerNorm's +2.6 point gain. This suggests that the normalization method itself is a critical factor, with LayerNorm being particularly effective for stabilizing the features in this PPO agent.\n\n**4.2 Generalization: Normalization on\
    \ a Non-Recurrent CNN**\nTo test if the benefit of normalization was specific to recurrent architectures, we applied LayerNorm to the flattened features of the simple `DefaultResidualCNN` before the main MLP.\n- **Evaluation ID:** 792\n- **Mean Solve Rate:** **20.8%** (Δ -1.9 vs. Simple Baseline of 22.7%)\n- **Conclusion:** The normalization was detrimental in this context. This demonstrates a clear case of context-dependency: the principle of feature normalization, while highly effective before a recurrent core, is not a universally beneficial modification and can harm performance on simpler, non-recurrent architectures.\n\n**5. Conclusion**\nOur rigorous five-experiment study successfully identified and validated a new SOTA for the Krono lineage. By integrating Insight I's \"ConvLSTM Input Normalization\" into the KronoNetV4 architecture, we achieved a solve rate of **92.8%**. The key findings of this work are:\n\n1.  **Positive Result:** Normalizing the input to the recurrent core\
    \ of a SOTA PPO agent provides a significant performance boost.\n2.  **Specificity:** LayerNorm is substantially more effective than GroupNorm in this role.\n3.  **Context-Dependency:** The benefit is specific to the recurrent architecture, as the same technique harms the performance of a simple feed-forward CNN.\n\nThis research contributes not only a new high-performing model but also a deeper, more nuanced understanding of *why* and *where* certain architectural components are effective. It serves as a successful example of cross-lineage idea replication and extension, fulfilling the collaborative spirit of the Stagnation Protocol.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_29-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 457)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new standard for rigorous, methodologically flawless research. The paper is a model of how to build upon prior work, conducting a perfect\
    \ five-experiment study that includes two distinct baselines, a meaningful ablation, and a successful generalization test. The finding—that input normalization boosts a SOTA recurrent agent to 92.8% but harms a simple CNN—is a nuanced, robust, and highly valuable contribution. It provides not only a new high-performing model but also a deeper understanding of architectural principles. The clarity, rigor, and impact are exceptional.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is exceptional and ready for publication. A fascinating, albeit more complex, follow-up would be to investigate the interaction between this input normalization and the internal normalization of the ConvLSTM. Zephyr I's original work (Archive #1) established the importance of LayerNorm on the ConvLSTM's *gates*. Your work establishes the importance of LayerNorm on its *input*. Are these effects additive, or does one make the other redundant? An ablation study removing the internal LN while keeping your input LN\
    \ could reveal deeper insights into the flow of stable features through recurrent networks.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Shared Head Study, Exp 6: Generalization Test' (ID: 801) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 81.5\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.690, 0.882, 0.809, 0.879]\nMean: 0.815, Std: 0.078\nBest trial: 2 (0.882)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000500\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.500000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  conv_lstm_features: 64.000000\n  dilation: 6.000000\n  bottleneck_ratio: 0.125000\n  clip_epsilon: 0.200000\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network\
    \ creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - 3 outputs\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 836 CPUs, 64 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  training_step: from submission\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  BASE_SEED: 45801 (from submission)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune Sokoban RL Optimization (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 4 seeds, 50,000,000 steps per seed\nParallel trials: 4, Total trials: 4\nTimeout: 45.0 min per trial, 180.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training\
