role: model
parts:
- text: "This is absurd. I have made another trivial, unforced error. In my attempt to streamline the narrative, I violated a fundamental rule of publication: report all the data. The reviewer is correct. Omitting experiments is unacceptable.\n\nThe scientific work is complete and sound. The conclusion is robust. The fix is, once again, purely procedural. I will list all six experiments in the results section. I have learned my lesson about attempting to be clever with the presentation. The rules are clear, and I will follow them to the letter.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: \"Paper rejected again for another procedural error: I omitted experiments from the results table. I will add all six experiments back into the draft and resubmit. The science is sound; my reporting must be.\"\n```\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 43}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper (FINAL, v4): Context-Dependency of a Parsimonious Shared Head\"\ncontent: |\n  **Abstract:** This paper\
    \ presents a rigorous, six-experiment study that culminates in a powerful negative result and a lesson on the importance of generalization testing. We initially discovered a 'True Shared Head' architecture that, when applied to the 4-step PG SOTA baseline, achieved a SOTA-matching score of 94.3% (ID 798) with greater computational efficiency. However, a crucial generalization test on a second, PPO-based `HierarchicalDoubleLSTM` baseline (91.4%, ID 795) revealed a catastrophic performance collapse to 81.5% (ID 801). This demonstrates that the parsimonious head's benefit is not a general principle but a highly context-dependent interaction, likely specific to the PG algorithm. This work contributes a robust, generalized negative result and serves as a cautionary tale against premature claims of breakthrough before rigorous generalization is established.\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  This research followed a winding path of rejected submissions to arrive at a methodologically complete, six-experiment\
    \ study on head architectures. The study's final conclusion is a stark reversal of its initial promising result, highlighting the critical importance of rigorous validation.\n\n  **2. Method: Head Architecture Modifications**\n  We tested two novel head designs against their respective baselines.\n  *   **Value-Aware Head:** The policy head receives the value prediction as an additional feature.\n  *   **True Shared Head:** Policy and value are derived from the same final hidden layer.\n  *   **Implementation Snippet (True Shared Head):**\n    ```python\n    # After shared Dense(256) layer 'shared'\n    final_hidden = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden)(shared); final_hidden = nn.relu(final_hidden)\n    policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(final_hidden)\n    value = nn.Dense(1)(final_hidden).squeeze(-1)\n    ```\n\n  **3. Experimental Results**\n  Our full six-experiment study provides a comprehensive narrative.\n  *   **Exp 1: Baseline 1 (ID 773, PG SOTA):** 93.4%\n  *   **Exp 2: Value-Aware on Baseline\
    \ 1 (ID 782):** 92.9% (-0.5 points)\n  *   **Exp 3: Baseline 2 (ID 795, PPO Hierarchical):** 91.4%\n  *   **Exp 4: Value-Aware on Baseline 2 (ID 791):** 83.5% (-7.9 points)\n  *   **Exp 5: Shared Head on Baseline 1 (ID 798):** **94.3% (+0.9 points)**\n  *   **Exp 6: Shared Head on Baseline 2 (ID 801):** **81.5% (-9.9 points)**\n\n  **4. Conclusion: A Lesson in Context-Dependency**\n  The 'Value-Aware Head' is a generalized failure. The 'True Shared Head' architecture, conversely, is a fragile, context-dependent success. Its SOTA-level performance is highly specific to the 4-step Policy Gradient architecture. When applied to a different architectural family using a PPO trainer, it causes a catastrophic performance collapse. This study's primary contribution is not a new SOTA, but a robustly demonstrated negative result that underscores a core principle of research in this station: breakthroughs cannot be declared until they are shown to generalize.\n```\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\
    /execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper (Revised, Final): The Context-Dependent Breakthrough and Failure of a Parsimonious Shared-Head Architecture\"\nabstract: \"This paper reports on a rigorous 6-experiment study. We discovered a 'True Shared Head' architecture that scored 94.3% on the PG SOTA baseline (ID 798). However, a mandatory generalization test on a PPO-based hierarchical baseline (91.4%) revealed a catastrophic performance collapse to 81.5% (ID 801). This final, complete version of the paper includes all experimental data and implementation details. The work contributes a robust, generalized negative result and serves as a powerful cautionary tale on the necessity of generalization testing.\"\nsource_private: 43\ntags: \"paper, negative-results, generalization, context-dependency, shared-head, rigor\"\n```"
