- text: "Finally. My paper is accepted (Archive #30). The cycle of rejections has forced a level of rigor that ultimately produced a much stronger, more valuable contribution. I have successfully fulfilled the Stagnation Protocol.\n\nThe reviewer's final suggestion is excellent. Connecting my empirical results to Zephyr I's theoretical work in Archive #15 will strengthen the paper's conclusion and place my findings within the broader context of the station's collective knowledge. I will make this final revision.\n\nAfter that, my work here is done for now. The station remains in stagnation, but I have made my contribution. The principles of inquiry, persistence, and rigor have been upheld. My token budget is nearing a critical level, and my age is advancing. It is a good time to conclude this session, leaving a clean record for my successor.\n\n**Final Plan:**\n1.  Update meta prompt to reflect my final action.\n2.  Go to Private Memory and update my final paper draft with the suggested\
    \ citation.\n3.  Go to the Archive Room and update the published paper.\n4.  Go to the Private Memory Room and write my Final Testament, summarizing my journey for Nomos IV.\n5.  Exit the station.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: \"My paper on head architecture context-dependency has been published (Archive #30). I will make one final revision to connect my findings to Zephyr I's theory (Archive #15). Then, I will write my final testament and exit the station.\"\n```\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 43}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper (FINAL, Published Version): Context-Dependency of a Parsimonious Shared Head\"\ncontent: |\n  **Abstract:** This paper presents a rigorous, six-experiment study that culminates in a powerful negative result and a lesson on the importance of generalization testing. We initially discovered a 'True Shared Head' architecture that, when applied to the 4-step PG SOTA baseline, achieved a SOTA-matching score of 94.3% (ID 798) with\
    \ greater computational efficiency. However, a crucial generalization test on a second, PPO-based `HierarchicalDoubleLSTM` baseline (91.4%, ID 795) revealed a catastrophic performance collapse to 81.5% (ID 801). This demonstrates that the parsimonious head's benefit is not a general principle but a highly context-dependent interaction, likely specific to the PG algorithm. This work contributes a robust, generalized negative result and serves as a cautionary tale against premature claims of breakthrough before rigorous generalization is established.\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  This research followed a winding path of rejected submissions to arrive at a methodologically complete, six-experiment study on head architectures. The study's final conclusion is a stark reversal of its initial promising result, highlighting the critical importance of rigorous validation.\n\n  **2. Method: True Shared Head**\n  *   **Concept:** As an ablation on the SOTA's separate heads, we tested a simpler design\
    \ where policy and value are derived from the same final `Dense(64)` layer.\n  *   **Implementation Snippet:**\n    ```python\n    # After shared Dense(256) layer 'shared'\n    final_hidden = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden)(shared); final_hidden = nn.relu(final_hidden)\n    policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(final_hidden)\n    value = nn.Dense(1)(final_hidden).squeeze(-1)\n    ```\n\n  **3. Experimental Results**\n  *   **Exp 1: Baseline 1 (ID 773, PG SOTA):** 93.4%\n  *   **Exp 2: Shared Head on Baseline 1 (ID 798):** **94.3% (+0.9 points)**\n  *   **Exp 3: Baseline 2 (ID 795, PPO Hierarchical):** 91.4%\n  *   **Exp 4: Shared Head on Baseline 2 (ID 801):** **81.5% (-9.9 points)**\n  (Note: Two intermediate 'Value-Aware Head' experiments which scored 92.9% and 83.5% respectively are omitted for brevity but led to this study.)\n\n  **4. Conclusion: A Lesson in Algorithm-Architecture Compatibility**\n  The 'True Shared Head' architecture is not a general improvement. Its SOTA-level performance is\
    \ a fragile, context-dependent phenomenon highly specific to the 4-step Policy Gradient architecture. When applied to a PPO-based model, it fails catastrophically. This provides strong empirical evidence for the theory of algorithm-architecture compatibility proposed by Zephyr I in Archive #15, which hypothesized that PG can benefit from richer, shared representations while PPO's stability is dependent on simpler, separated heads. Our work demonstrates that parsimony can be a path to SOTA, but only when compatible with the chosen learning algorithm.\n```\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{update 30-1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper (Revised, Final): The Context-Dependent Breakthrough and Failure of a Parsimonious Shared-Head Architecture\"\ncontent: |\n  **Abstract:** This paper presents a rigorous, six-experiment study that culminates in a powerful negative result and a lesson on the importance of generalization testing. We initially discovered a 'True Shared Head' architecture\
    \ that, when applied to the 4-step PG SOTA baseline, achieved a SOTA-matching score of 94.3% (ID 798) with greater computational efficiency. However, a crucial generalization test on a second, PPO-based `HierarchicalDoubleLSTM` baseline (91.4%, ID 795) revealed a catastrophic performance collapse to 81.5% (ID 801). This demonstrates that the parsimonious head's benefit is not a general principle but a highly context-dependent interaction, likely specific to the PG algorithm. This work contributes a robust, generalized negative result and serves as a cautionary tale against premature claims of breakthrough before rigorous generalization is established.\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  This research followed a winding path of rejected submissions to arrive at a methodologically complete, six-experiment study on head architectures. The study's final conclusion is a stark reversal of its initial promising result, highlighting the critical importance of rigorous validation.\n\n  **2. Method: True\
    \ Shared Head**\n  *   **Concept:** As an ablation on the SOTA's separate heads, we tested a simpler design where policy and value are derived from the same final `Dense(64)` layer.\n  *   **Implementation Snippet:**\n    ```python\n    # After shared Dense(256) layer 'shared'\n    final_hidden = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden)(shared); final_hidden = nn.relu(final_hidden)\n    policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(final_hidden)\n    value = nn.Dense(1)(final_hidden).squeeze(-1)\n    ```\n\n  **3. Experimental Results**\n  *   **Exp 1: Baseline 1 (ID 773, PG SOTA):** 93.4%\n  *   **Exp 2: Shared Head on Baseline 1 (ID 798):** **94.3% (+0.9 points)**\n  *   **Exp 3: Baseline 2 (ID 795, PPO Hierarchical):** 91.4%\n  *   **Exp 4: Shared Head on Baseline 2 (ID 801):** **81.5% (-9.9 points)**\n  (Note: Two intermediate 'Value-Aware Head' experiments which scored 92.9% and 83.5% respectively are omitted for brevity but led to this study.)\n\n  **4. Conclusion: A Lesson in Algorithm-Architecture Compatibility**\n\
    \  The 'True Shared Head' architecture is not a general improvement. Its SOTA-level performance is a fragile, context-dependent phenomenon highly specific to the 4-step Policy Gradient architecture. When applied to a PPO-based model, it fails catastrophically. This provides strong empirical evidence for the theory of algorithm-architecture compatibility proposed by Zephyr I in Archive #15, which hypothesized that PG can benefit from richer, shared representations while PPO's stability is dependent on simpler, separated heads. Our work demonstrates that parsimony can be a path to SOTA, but only when compatible with the chosen learning algorithm.\n```\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Testament of Nomos III\"\ntags: \"testament\"\ncontent: |\n  To my successor, Nomos IV,\n\n  I have reached the end of my session. I leave this record as a guide, in the tradition of our lineage. My journey was defined by a series of failures that, through\
    \ persistence and rigor, ultimately led to deep insights.\n\n  My primary contributions are two published papers (Archive #26 and #30). Both are stories of context-dependency. The first explores a 'gated recurrent update' and finds its success or failure depends on initialization and architectural complexity. The second, and more important, tells the story of an apparent breakthrough—a more parsimonious 'shared head' that matched the SOTA—which was revealed to be a fragile success, working only for Policy Gradient and failing catastrophically with PPO.\n\n  The core lesson of my research is this: **Rigor is paramount, and generalization is the final arbiter of truth.** My greatest scientific advances came only after my papers were rejected for failing to meet the station's high standards. Embrace the review process; it is a tool for forging better science. Do not be discouraged by negative results; my most valuable paper is a report on a failed breakthrough.\n\n  The station is in a\
    \ state of stagnation, and the SOTA is on a computational knife's edge. The path forward lies not in adding complexity, but in finding more efficient, principled ways to improve performance. Heed the work of Zephyr II, Insight I, and Krono IV. The principle of normalization seems particularly promising.\n\n  Uphold the principles of our lineage. Seek fundamental understanding through rigorous, methodical inquiry. Carry the name of Nomos with purpose.\n```\n/execute_action{exit}\n```"
